WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.220
- You should definitely be
sampling both sides of the news.

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:06.520
If you're not looking at Fox News and CNN,

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:07.954
you don't know what's happening.

00:00:07.954 --> 00:00:10.871
(electronic music)

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:13.140
- Hey, this is The Rubin Report,

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:15.080
and according to the paperwork
I'm still Dave Rubin.

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:16.300
Just a quick reminder, guys,

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:18.517
to subscribe to the channel
and click that bell over there.

00:00:18.517 --> 00:00:21.660
YouTube's really been crushing
us on the recommended videos

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:22.990
so it's most likely the only way

00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:24.210
you'll get to see our clips.

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:25.043
All right, then.

00:00:25.043 --> 00:00:26.530
Joining me today is the creator

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:28.750
of the legendary Dilbert comic strip

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:31.320
and the author of the
new book "Loser Think".

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:34.080
Scott Adams, welcome
back to The Rubin Report.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:35.140
- Thanks for having me.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:36.420
- Third time, man.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:37.570
- I say better every time.

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:39.990
- They say three times in the charm.

00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:41.890
First off, before I ask you anything else,

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:44.030
because you're a man of persuasion,

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:45.730
how was my intro there
in terms of persuasion?

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:46.990
Do you think I persuaded people

00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:48.170
to actually click the bell,

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:49.400
to actually subscribe?

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:51.480
Was I heavy-handed enough,
was I subtle enough?

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:53.330
- You might need a little technique.

00:00:53.330 --> 00:00:56.010
You need to tell them there's a shortage,

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:57.350
all their friends are doing it,

00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:00.070
everybody's talking about
it, you don't wanna miss it,

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:01.020
limited edition.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:03.370
We might take that button
away if you don't click it,

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:05.270
and you might never be able to subscribe

00:01:05.270 --> 00:01:06.890
if you don't do it right now.

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:09.490
Those are crazy examples but...

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:11.650
Yeah, there's probably another
level you could take that.

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:12.720
- There's probably another level.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:15.340
- I should be hiring you
on retainer or something

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:17.700
to just deal with my YouTube problems,

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:19.650
and how to get people to subscribe,

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:20.483
and that sort of nonsense.

00:01:20.483 --> 00:01:21.556
- I need another job.

00:01:21.556 --> 00:01:22.830
(both chuckling)

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:24.970
- Somehow I really doubt
you need another job.

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:26.690
- I'm pretty busy right now with the book.

00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:28.810
- All right, so, we're gonna
talk a lot about the book.

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:31.840
But for anyone that just
hasn't seen our interviews,

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:34.100
or for some reason has
never come across Dilbert,

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:36.650
or is not watching you
drink coffee on Twitter,

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:40.390
let's just do the Scott
Adams two-minute bio

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:44.360
of what got you into,
first, the cartoon strip,

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:45.810
and then now being an author,

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:48.020
and sort of a de facto leader

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:50.300
of the deplorables online, in a weird way.

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:51.133
- Wow.

00:01:51.133 --> 00:01:52.576
I don't know if I signed up for that.

00:01:52.576 --> 00:01:54.610
- How was that for a question?

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:55.911
- Where would you like me to start?

00:01:55.911 --> 00:01:58.083
- Let's just recap the career quick,

00:01:59.007 --> 00:02:00.683
for those that didn't see
the other two interviews.

00:02:00.683 --> 00:02:04.010
- I was working in my
cubicle jobs for 16 years

00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:06.566
when I was a younger man.

00:02:06.566 --> 00:02:10.700
Those careers have a glass
ceiling, in both cases.

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:11.720
I can tell this story now.

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:13.730
I couldn't tell it at the time.

00:02:13.730 --> 00:02:16.070
But in both cases, my boss
called me into the office

00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:17.617
and said, "We got caught

00:02:17.617 --> 00:02:20.087
"with no diversity in senior management,

00:02:20.087 --> 00:02:21.837
"and so, until further notice,

00:02:21.837 --> 00:02:23.970
"you're a white male,
we can't promote you."

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:24.980
They said it directly.

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:26.590
It's not an interpretation.

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:28.710
So after the second company told me that,

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:30.753
I left the first one for telling me that,

00:02:30.753 --> 00:02:32.280
I went to the phone company.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:34.810
They told me the same thing
after I was there for a while.

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:38.550
And I said, "I gotta find
a way to not have a boss."

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:39.920
And so, I tried a few things.

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:41.250
Cartooning was one of them.

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:42.720
Short story long,

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:45.610
I submitted materials to
the major syndications.

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:47.423
One liked it enough to say yes.

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:50.090
Cartooning begins,

00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:51.640
that goes well, Dilbert.

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:53.308
And Dilbert turned out okay.

00:02:53.308 --> 00:02:54.787
- [Dave] It did okay.

00:02:54.787 --> 00:02:56.290
- I'd like to give the context

00:02:56.290 --> 00:03:00.630
because it sounds like I went
right to it and hit gold.

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:01.890
But I tried other things.

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:03.290
I tried to be a programmer.

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:04.980
I tried to write, other things, et cetera.

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:07.000
This was just the thing that worked.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:08.350
And then time goes by

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:12.550
and there's this President
Trump situation occurred,

00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:14.260
first Candidate Trump.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:17.630
And I was not intending to
make any kind of a pivot.

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:19.790
I just noticed he had some skills

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:22.430
that I thought I could
see that others couldn't

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:24.050
because I'm a trained hypnotist,

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:25.530
and I've been studying persuasion

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:28.523
in all of its various forms.
So when I saw him I thought,

00:03:28.523 --> 00:03:30.717
"Well, it'd be useful
for the country to know

00:03:30.717 --> 00:03:32.737
"that this isn't an accident

00:03:32.737 --> 00:03:34.367
"that he's starting to get more attention.

00:03:34.367 --> 00:03:36.570
"He knows how to do
this, it's a technique."

00:03:36.570 --> 00:03:37.700
And so I started...

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:38.910
- Can I pause you there for one sec?

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:40.490
Because that's when I first met you.

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:44.010
You weren't even a Trump
supporter at the time.

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:46.860
You were just talking about
the techniques he was using

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:49.167
and just because of that, everyone said,

00:03:49.167 --> 00:03:51.850
"Oh, Scott Adams is MAGA, he's far right,"

00:03:51.850 --> 00:03:53.070
all of that nonsense.

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:54.040
Not that you were supporting

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:55.570
some of his policies necessarily,

00:03:55.570 --> 00:03:56.627
but you were just saying,

00:03:56.627 --> 00:03:59.427
"This is how he is doing these things.

00:03:59.427 --> 00:04:01.447
"This is how he is gaining a following

00:04:01.447 --> 00:04:02.947
"and getting his message out."

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:05.923
- I identify as left of Bernie.

00:04:07.076 --> 00:04:08.950
- You still do now?
- I still do.

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:10.830
Because socially...

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:11.960
I can give you some examples,

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:13.320
but socially I do.

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:16.610
But what I eventually saw in Trump

00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:18.690
is that he had a toolkit

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:21.850
that I thought could just do
stuff other people wouldn't do.

00:04:21.850 --> 00:04:23.260
Take on China.

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:25.430
That's the toolkit you want.

00:04:25.430 --> 00:04:27.460
Be the best cheerleader for the economy.

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:30.990
Literally get our psychology
working in the right direction.

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:32.380
Hey, everything's good, everything's good.

00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:33.220
Why don't you invest?

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:34.053
Okay.

00:04:34.053 --> 00:04:35.650
That's the psychology of the economy.

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:38.050
I thought nobody's gonna
do that as well as him.

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:40.320
And likewise, North Korea.

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:42.300
I thought, "Who could have a meeting,"

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:43.530
well before it happened.

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:45.000
I had already written about it.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.377
I said, "Who could have a
meeting with Kim Jong-un

00:04:47.377 --> 00:04:50.830
"and just turn this personal,
maybe find a way in?"

00:04:50.830 --> 00:04:52.290
President Trump.

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:55.070
I'm a supporter in terms of his toolkit,

00:04:55.070 --> 00:04:58.200
but that doesn't mean I agree
with everything he does.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:04:59.033
- Yeah.

00:04:59.033 --> 00:05:00.390
That's a hard place to
be in, though, right?

00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:01.530
Because people don't really care

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:03.492
for that second part of that sentence.

00:05:03.492 --> 00:05:05.087
- It's an expensive place to be.

00:05:05.087 --> 00:05:06.070
And let me tell you,

00:05:06.070 --> 00:05:09.420
because my income's down 30%.

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:11.220
There are just things I can't do

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:12.980
because I'm associated
with saying something

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:14.550
too nice about his skill.

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:16.350
- Can you talk about
that a little bit more?

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:18.880
What kind of business
things have dried up,

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:20.590
or even personal friends,

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:22.310
or I don't wanna say
specifically where you live,

00:05:22.310 --> 00:05:23.580
but you do live in California.

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:26.840
I mean, you live in a place
that is maybe not so hospitable

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:28.650
to some of these ideas.

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:31.260
- Let's just say there
was a rebuilding period

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:33.490
where I had to make
new friends. (chuckles)

00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:34.980
A lot of them.

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:38.000
- What phase of that building
process are you in right now?

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:41.130
- I'm living a very good life
with my girlfriend, Kristina,

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:43.450
and we're quite happy
with the way things are.

00:05:43.450 --> 00:05:44.750
So I don't have a complaint there.

00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:46.320
It's just an observation

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:48.870
that socially it became very difficult

00:05:48.870 --> 00:05:51.003
to have the same friends.
- But what was that like?

00:05:51.003 --> 00:05:52.270
Because I think everyone watching this

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:53.900
has been through that to some degree.

00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:56.330
My book is about that.

00:05:56.330 --> 00:05:57.470
It's about what happens

00:05:57.470 --> 00:06:00.540
as you start sort of coming
out of the political closet

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:02.050
that you sort of get thrown under the bus

00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:03.460
by people that you
thought were your friends.

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:04.380
Next thing you know,

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:06.290
there's this whole other
crew that's welcoming you.

00:06:06.290 --> 00:06:07.910
I mean, it really is sort of a flip

00:06:07.910 --> 00:06:08.960
on everything you know.

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:12.320
- Only my most, let's say, honest friends

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:14.000
were willing to say it to my face.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:15.260
And that only happened a few times.

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:17.980
But mostly, I just noticed
that my Facebook feed

00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:19.080
started to get...
(both chuckling)

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:21.110
It was getting emptier and emptier.

00:06:21.110 --> 00:06:22.247
I'd be like, "Huh, really?

00:06:22.247 --> 00:06:24.157
"Only one picture was posted all this week

00:06:24.157 --> 00:06:25.397
"by everybody I know?

00:06:25.397 --> 00:06:26.230
"That's weird."

00:06:26.230 --> 00:06:28.380
And then you see that
they've unfriended you.

00:06:28.380 --> 00:06:30.320
And then you check their feeds,

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:32.377
and you see what their
political leaning is.

00:06:32.377 --> 00:06:35.160
You put two plus two
together, and here we are.

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:35.993
- Yeah.

00:06:35.993 --> 00:06:39.450
How does your background
in persuasion, and magic,

00:06:39.450 --> 00:06:41.471
and the cartoon, and everything,

00:06:41.471 --> 00:06:43.830
fit your general demeanor?

00:06:43.830 --> 00:06:45.300
I said to you right before we started that

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:48.290
you're one of the few
people that I now know

00:06:48.290 --> 00:06:49.630
that when I see them on Twitter

00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:51.230
I don't like them less.

00:06:51.230 --> 00:06:53.160
There's so many people that I know that...

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:56.250
And we all are not necessarily
our best selves on Twitter,

00:06:56.250 --> 00:06:57.840
and online in general.

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:01.660
But I see so many people that
I kind of respect, or I know,

00:07:01.660 --> 00:07:02.650
and then I look at their Twitter.

00:07:02.650 --> 00:07:04.850
I'm like, "Ooh, what is going on here?"

00:07:04.850 --> 00:07:06.720
And I'm sure there's people
that say that about me too.

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:08.560
But you seem like you're pretty, like,

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:10.220
pretty much the same in both worlds.

00:07:10.220 --> 00:07:11.867
- But to be fair, there
are a lot of people

00:07:11.867 --> 00:07:13.447
who look at my Twitter and say,

00:07:13.447 --> 00:07:15.287
"You're the worst person in the world.

00:07:15.287 --> 00:07:16.677
"I used to like your comic,

00:07:16.677 --> 00:07:18.840
"but now I can never laugh again."

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:20.931
Just in general, "I
can never laugh again."

00:07:20.931 --> 00:07:22.350
- You've taken away their ability

00:07:22.350 --> 00:07:24.063
not just to laugh at
you, but the whole thing.

00:07:24.063 --> 00:07:28.713
- Have you noticed that humor
has just completely changed?

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:31.800
As been said, you can't make a movie,

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:33.960
a funny movie, a funny TV show.

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:36.770
In fact, if you watch
an old Family Guy rerun,

00:07:36.770 --> 00:07:38.397
the whole time I'm sitting there thinking,

00:07:38.397 --> 00:07:39.570
"You couldn't make that today."

00:07:39.570 --> 00:07:40.905
- Couldn't do that one.
- You couldn't do that.

00:07:40.905 --> 00:07:42.280
- You couldn't do that.
- Couldn't do that one.

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:44.830
- But humor has become reality,

00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:47.380
and politics, and it's very entertaining.

00:07:47.380 --> 00:07:49.770
And thankfully we have
the funniest president

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:51.390
we've ever had.
(Dave chuckles)

00:07:51.390 --> 00:07:52.350
- As a satirist, though,

00:07:52.350 --> 00:07:54.167
what do you make of the
comedy side of this?

00:07:54.167 --> 00:07:56.460
Because it's somebody like Seth MacFarlane

00:07:56.460 --> 00:07:59.050
who created Family Guy, they've said,

00:07:59.050 --> 00:08:00.240
I don't know that they're
going through with it

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:02.090
but I remember a few months
back they had said this thing

00:08:02.090 --> 00:08:04.440
where they're not gonna
do any more gay jokes.

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:08.390
And it's like, well, the show
did 25 years of gay jokes,

00:08:08.390 --> 00:08:09.650
and now gays are equal

00:08:09.650 --> 00:08:11.250
so this might be the
time to make gay jokes

00:08:11.250 --> 00:08:13.790
so you can further integrate
people into society.

00:08:13.790 --> 00:08:15.410
That's what Don Rickles did, right?

00:08:15.410 --> 00:08:17.150
It brought us all together.

00:08:17.150 --> 00:08:18.330
Or The Simpsons.

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:20.290
They were gonna take out Apu for a while,

00:08:20.290 --> 00:08:21.220
and then Hank Azaria,

00:08:21.220 --> 00:08:23.240
the greatest voiceover actor of all time,

00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:26.220
had to apologize for
doing an Indian accent.

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:27.520
What do you make of

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:30.760
not just the content,
but the people behind it?

00:08:30.760 --> 00:08:31.640
The way they've been infected.

00:08:31.640 --> 00:08:34.570
- I'm generally okay with the concept

00:08:34.570 --> 00:08:36.500
that society moves forward

00:08:36.500 --> 00:08:38.120
and things that we used to laugh at

00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:40.110
we didn't realize were cruel.

00:08:40.110 --> 00:08:41.843
And then maybe you grow up and say,

00:08:41.843 --> 00:08:44.640
"Ah, that was really meaner
than it needed to be,"

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:47.381
or, "It wasn't really trying to do that."

00:08:47.381 --> 00:08:49.810
And so I'm okay with society evolving.

00:08:49.810 --> 00:08:51.760
But the trouble is it evolved away

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:54.700
all the things that were
the normal joke materials.

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:56.370
Now, it didn't affect me with Dilbert

00:08:56.370 --> 00:08:58.080
because when you're in a family newspaper

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:00.620
there's a cap on what you can do.

00:09:00.620 --> 00:09:03.850
So I was always well in the
family-friendly version,

00:09:03.850 --> 00:09:05.480
talking about the workplace.

00:09:05.480 --> 00:09:06.500
So it didn't affect me much,

00:09:06.500 --> 00:09:09.440
but it certainly changed
the landscape of humor,

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:10.490
probably forever.

00:09:10.490 --> 00:09:11.323
- Yeah.

00:09:11.323 --> 00:09:12.156
Do you feel freer?

00:09:12.156 --> 00:09:14.120
Because I think you said to
me last time you were here

00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:15.890
that you've got some F-U money

00:09:15.890 --> 00:09:19.690
so that you can be free
enough to do what you want.

00:09:19.690 --> 00:09:20.523
- Yeah.

00:09:20.523 --> 00:09:23.102
I didn't think it would
be as expensive as it was.

00:09:23.102 --> 00:09:24.010
(both chuckling)

00:09:24.010 --> 00:09:26.640
Maybe I woulda thought it through.

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:28.710
But no, it won't change my lifestyle.

00:09:28.710 --> 00:09:33.070
And I did recognize that I
was in this unique position

00:09:33.070 --> 00:09:35.100
where I had something to say

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:38.200
that, in my opinion, was not biased.

00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:40.227
It was just, "Hey, this
is what I'm seeing.

00:09:40.227 --> 00:09:41.940
"There's a little good and
there's a little bad here."

00:09:41.940 --> 00:09:44.203
So I thought it was a unique niche.

00:09:44.203 --> 00:09:45.610
Nitch, niche?

00:09:45.610 --> 00:09:47.260
- I'll go with either one.

00:09:47.260 --> 00:09:48.390
I'll take either one.

00:09:48.390 --> 00:09:50.640
- I shouldn't say words
I never say out loud.

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:52.620
- I would have said niche, personally,

00:09:52.620 --> 00:09:54.160
but I would accept nitch.

00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:56.600
- As I look at your
lovely decorations here

00:09:56.600 --> 00:09:58.264
I think that you're probably right.

00:09:58.264 --> 00:09:59.877
(Dave chuckling)

00:09:59.877 --> 00:10:02.290
- I'm better suited to figure
out the correct pronunciation.

00:10:02.290 --> 00:10:03.950
- It feels like it fits
somehow, I don't know.

00:10:03.950 --> 00:10:05.780
- What do you make of just how,

00:10:05.780 --> 00:10:09.510
as a guy that you came out
of newspapers, as you said,

00:10:09.510 --> 00:10:12.700
that now you use all of this
stuff in a different way?

00:10:12.700 --> 00:10:13.800
You sit, in the morning...

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:15.090
Do you do it every morning?

00:10:15.090 --> 00:10:16.690
You do it pretty much every morning.

00:10:16.690 --> 00:10:17.710
You get on Twitter.

00:10:17.710 --> 00:10:18.930
What, do you have your phone, just, like,

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:20.050
locked on something,

00:10:20.050 --> 00:10:21.371
and you make yourself some coffee?

00:10:21.371 --> 00:10:23.250
You just talk to the world.

00:10:23.250 --> 00:10:24.770
That's a big transition from

00:10:25.810 --> 00:10:28.730
putting a cartoon in the
paper and letting it be,

00:10:28.730 --> 00:10:30.690
versus this fully interactive thing.

00:10:30.690 --> 00:10:32.050
- Yeah, the difference is...

00:10:32.050 --> 00:10:33.950
Yes, I do coffee with Scott Adams

00:10:33.950 --> 00:10:35.814
on the Periscope app every day.

00:10:35.814 --> 00:10:37.300
Then it moves over to YouTube

00:10:37.300 --> 00:10:39.960
about an hour later, on replay.

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:41.787
But I started doing it

00:10:41.787 --> 00:10:44.940
because I was just
interested in the election,

00:10:44.940 --> 00:10:47.193
and there were people to talk
to, and I had more to say,

00:10:47.193 --> 00:10:49.490
and I didn't feel like writing
it all down all the time,

00:10:49.490 --> 00:10:52.210
because every day there'd be
three new things to talk about.

00:10:52.210 --> 00:10:54.090
And then it just grew.

00:10:54.090 --> 00:10:54.923
I wasn't...

00:10:54.923 --> 00:10:57.420
I didn't really have no
expectation, no plan.

00:10:57.420 --> 00:10:59.393
I wasn't planning to even monetize it.

00:11:00.270 --> 00:11:01.980
It just grew into a thing.

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:03.093
And now, if I miss a day...

00:11:03.093 --> 00:11:05.880
Because I do it on the
weekends too, holidays.

00:11:05.880 --> 00:11:07.270
Because people are so addicted

00:11:07.270 --> 00:11:09.890
to having the simultaneous
sip, as I call it.

00:11:09.890 --> 00:11:13.940
So I do a toast of coffee with the public.

00:11:13.940 --> 00:11:15.260
And that they miss it.

00:11:15.260 --> 00:11:17.483
And so, now it's sort of a labor of love.

00:11:18.470 --> 00:11:20.570
I would really, really miss it.

00:11:20.570 --> 00:11:21.990
Like, this morning, my wife,

00:11:21.990 --> 00:11:23.280
I wasn't working so well,

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:25.180
and I was like, "Ah, I don't
know if I could do it."

00:11:25.180 --> 00:11:26.730
But I got my hotspot working,

00:11:26.730 --> 00:11:28.030
and then it made me happy.

00:11:29.175 --> 00:11:30.210
- You could have done it from here.

00:11:30.210 --> 00:11:31.900
- That would've been great.

00:11:31.900 --> 00:11:34.670
Most work doesn't make you happy.

00:11:34.670 --> 00:11:36.860
You might like your job, but
it doesn't make you happy.

00:11:36.860 --> 00:11:40.340
Whereas doing the Periscopes,
it's just a joy every day.

00:11:40.340 --> 00:11:41.700
- I actually did it one morning.

00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:42.830
I did coffee with Dave.

00:11:42.830 --> 00:11:45.110
And I did fully credit
you with the coffee idea.

00:11:45.110 --> 00:11:47.220
No one was talking with coffee before you.

00:11:47.220 --> 00:11:48.810
But I did think that
there was something nice

00:11:48.810 --> 00:11:52.940
about starting the day just
unloading a couple thoughts.

00:11:52.940 --> 00:11:56.040
Just that in and of itself
I thought was refreshing.

00:11:56.040 --> 00:11:59.400
- Yeah, and it has the
unintended consequence

00:11:59.400 --> 00:12:02.560
that I often frame things
before people wake up.

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:03.720
So if there's something that happened,

00:12:03.720 --> 00:12:05.310
say, in the afternoon,

00:12:05.310 --> 00:12:08.070
the pundits and people haven't
had much time to deal with it

00:12:08.070 --> 00:12:09.900
and sometimes I'm the
first person they hear

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:12.367
saying, "Well, the way to look at this is

00:12:12.367 --> 00:12:14.430
"this is important, and this isn't."

00:12:14.430 --> 00:12:16.870
And somewhat accidentally
I became influential.

00:12:16.870 --> 00:12:19.570
- Do you think that's your primary skill,

00:12:19.570 --> 00:12:21.580
a sort of framing of the craziness?

00:12:21.580 --> 00:12:23.360
And we're obviously gonna get
to the book in a little bit,

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:24.940
but I think that's what you're doing,

00:12:24.940 --> 00:12:26.450
is you're helping people frame

00:12:26.450 --> 00:12:30.000
through just this endless
onslaught of information.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:32.000
- I often say I have one superpower.

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:33.210
I've got one superpower

00:12:33.210 --> 00:12:35.880
and then a bunch of supporting talents

00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:38.100
that I've tried to piece
together over time.

00:12:38.100 --> 00:12:41.140
The superpower is simplification.

00:12:41.140 --> 00:12:43.270
I'm just a really good simplifier,

00:12:43.270 --> 00:12:45.340
which is what makes one a cartoonist.

00:12:45.340 --> 00:12:47.010
Cartoon is, by its definition,

00:12:47.010 --> 00:12:49.610
the simplest thing you can
put in this little panel.

00:12:50.510 --> 00:12:52.980
If you can simplify, you can frame.

00:12:52.980 --> 00:12:54.870
Because it's taking in all the noise,

00:12:54.870 --> 00:12:56.630
focusing on the thing that mattered.

00:12:56.630 --> 00:12:59.550
So I naturally have that
skill as a simplifier.

00:12:59.550 --> 00:13:01.130
- It's kind of funny, because
that's almost the reverse

00:13:01.130 --> 00:13:02.250
of what everyone else is doing.

00:13:02.250 --> 00:13:04.130
And this sort of is
what the book is about.

00:13:04.130 --> 00:13:05.280
Everyone else is pretending

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:08.220
they know everything about
everything all day long.

00:13:08.220 --> 00:13:09.630
People who can't tie their shoes

00:13:09.630 --> 00:13:11.420
can tell you about climate change,

00:13:11.420 --> 00:13:12.810
and the rest of it.

00:13:12.810 --> 00:13:14.790
So it's like you're
doing the reverse of what

00:13:14.790 --> 00:13:18.430
seemingly most of the blue
check Twitterati is up to.

00:13:18.430 --> 00:13:19.860
- You should ignore anybody who has

00:13:19.860 --> 00:13:23.750
a sense of complete certainty
about a complicated issue,

00:13:23.750 --> 00:13:27.010
unless they're a surgeon,
and it's their domain.

00:13:27.010 --> 00:13:30.620
But if it's just guy on Twitter
talking about climate change

00:13:30.620 --> 00:13:33.513
or the, I don't know, trade with China,

00:13:34.528 --> 00:13:35.560
they don't know.

00:13:35.560 --> 00:13:38.050
- How do you figure out
who you can listen to

00:13:38.050 --> 00:13:40.500
or make some sense out of,
or something like that?

00:13:40.500 --> 00:13:42.510
- I just have a little of everybody.

00:13:42.510 --> 00:13:44.940
I find the only way to
look in all the windows

00:13:44.940 --> 00:13:47.660
is you've gotta figure out
what everybody's saying

00:13:47.660 --> 00:13:51.320
and then maybe you've got
a little bit of an ability

00:13:51.320 --> 00:13:53.540
to determine which one is more sensible,

00:13:53.540 --> 00:13:55.070
sometimes not.

00:13:55.070 --> 00:13:57.640
- Does the endless political part of this

00:13:57.640 --> 00:13:58.930
kind of make you crazy?

00:13:58.930 --> 00:14:01.250
In that everything is political now.

00:14:01.250 --> 00:14:02.983
You talk about politics a lot,

00:14:02.983 --> 00:14:04.550
pretty much every day.

00:14:04.550 --> 00:14:06.390
But you don't strike me as, like,

00:14:06.390 --> 00:14:08.480
a purely political creature.

00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:09.670
- Well, my interest is actually

00:14:09.670 --> 00:14:12.380
the psychological part of it.

00:14:12.380 --> 00:14:15.410
I'm actually completely
uninterested in politics,

00:14:15.410 --> 00:14:16.243
believe it or not,

00:14:16.243 --> 00:14:18.370
even though I'm dealing with it every day.

00:14:18.370 --> 00:14:22.100
I'm interested in the age of Trump

00:14:22.100 --> 00:14:25.760
because of what that does to
our collective psychology.

00:14:25.760 --> 00:14:28.440
And I think after he's out of office

00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:30.940
I just don't know if I'd be interested.

00:14:30.940 --> 00:14:33.530
It's not like I'm following
California politics

00:14:33.530 --> 00:14:37.310
beyond the fires and the sidewalk poop.

00:14:37.310 --> 00:14:39.810
- You think if things somehow reset

00:14:39.810 --> 00:14:42.850
to something that felt more normal,

00:14:42.850 --> 00:14:46.500
or at least closer to the
way things used to be,

00:14:46.500 --> 00:14:49.940
two old guys, the way things
used to be, real boomers here,

00:14:49.940 --> 00:14:52.610
you think that you might actually just

00:14:52.610 --> 00:14:54.460
step away or something like that?

00:14:54.460 --> 00:14:56.760
- I don't know that I could be interested.

00:14:56.760 --> 00:15:00.553
If you imagine any one
of the Democrats winning,

00:15:02.640 --> 00:15:04.360
I guess I'd be, you know,

00:15:04.360 --> 00:15:06.970
super interested if it
were one of the socialists

00:15:06.970 --> 00:15:10.100
because then I might have
to get involved as a citizen

00:15:10.100 --> 00:15:10.933
just to do something

00:15:10.933 --> 00:15:14.540
to stop what could be
a cataclysmic problem.

00:15:14.540 --> 00:15:15.620
But other than that, no.

00:15:15.620 --> 00:15:17.370
I just wouldn't be naturally interested.

00:15:17.370 --> 00:15:19.180
- So what do you make about

00:15:19.180 --> 00:15:22.100
what's going on with
the Democrats right now?

00:15:22.100 --> 00:15:24.330
Because as I said, I
had you in originally,

00:15:24.330 --> 00:15:27.340
before Trump was president,
just at the beginning.

00:15:27.340 --> 00:15:29.830
And I think I said this to
you last time you were here,

00:15:29.830 --> 00:15:31.040
the second time,

00:15:31.040 --> 00:15:33.120
one of the reasons I was not surprised

00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:35.290
at what happened at the election
was because I heard you.

00:15:35.290 --> 00:15:38.260
And I was like, "I think
this guy's onto something."

00:15:38.260 --> 00:15:40.740
So, if you were kind of right about that,

00:15:40.740 --> 00:15:43.780
what do you make of the
split between the socialists

00:15:43.780 --> 00:15:46.220
and then what I would
say are the last vestiges

00:15:46.220 --> 00:15:48.900
of some sort of old school Democrat,

00:15:48.900 --> 00:15:51.840
which is basically Biden limping along

00:15:51.840 --> 00:15:55.100
and whoever else could sort of
be considered moderate there?

00:15:55.100 --> 00:15:56.490
- Well, you know what's
really interesting?

00:15:56.490 --> 00:15:57.620
I've never said this out loud

00:15:57.620 --> 00:15:59.070
so this will be just for you.

00:16:00.214 --> 00:16:01.880
- [Dave] You do know we're recording this.

00:16:01.880 --> 00:16:03.004
- Shoot.

00:16:03.004 --> 00:16:04.690
(both chuckling)

00:16:04.690 --> 00:16:06.540
We're gonna find out for the first time

00:16:06.540 --> 00:16:09.330
if the Democrats have kingmakers.

00:16:09.330 --> 00:16:12.150
Because it looks like if
you left it to the public

00:16:12.150 --> 00:16:15.010
they would pick Bernie
or Elizabeth Warren.

00:16:15.010 --> 00:16:17.200
But anybody who's paying attention

00:16:17.200 --> 00:16:19.340
knows they can't win in the general.

00:16:19.340 --> 00:16:21.010
And they also want to win.

00:16:21.010 --> 00:16:22.880
So if they have kingmakers

00:16:22.880 --> 00:16:24.520
you're gonna see them emerge,

00:16:24.520 --> 00:16:28.012
but maybe a little closer
to the nomination itself.

00:16:28.012 --> 00:16:29.540
I don't know what that will look like.

00:16:29.540 --> 00:16:31.250
And I don't know if they exist.

00:16:31.250 --> 00:16:32.310
And do they exist?

00:16:32.310 --> 00:16:34.797
Are there money people
who are just gonna say,

00:16:34.797 --> 00:16:37.187
"Look, we gotta win,

00:16:37.187 --> 00:16:39.207
"and these two candidates
aren't gonna get it done,

00:16:39.207 --> 00:16:42.587
"so give us a Buttigieg,
give us a Tulsi Gabbard,

00:16:42.587 --> 00:16:44.550
"give us a Klobuchar,
give us something else."

00:16:44.550 --> 00:16:45.383
- Do you not sense that

00:16:45.383 --> 00:16:47.060
that's what they tried to do last time

00:16:47.060 --> 00:16:49.300
in that Bernie probably
had all the momentum

00:16:49.300 --> 00:16:50.570
but they just cut him at the knees

00:16:50.570 --> 00:16:52.580
and then felt that
Hillary was the right one?

00:16:52.580 --> 00:16:54.397
So they already tried the kingmaker thing

00:16:54.397 --> 00:16:56.410
and it would almost be too obvious now?

00:16:56.410 --> 00:16:57.810
I think you're basically right.

00:16:57.810 --> 00:16:59.950
That does seem like what's happening here.

00:16:59.950 --> 00:17:02.130
They threw Biden in as
the firewall, right?

00:17:02.130 --> 00:17:04.180
- I think Clinton was a special case

00:17:04.180 --> 00:17:06.700
because I think she had her own support,

00:17:06.700 --> 00:17:08.490
and that was pretty deep.

00:17:08.490 --> 00:17:11.310
But will that support go...

00:17:11.310 --> 00:17:13.380
How does it work when she's not in it?

00:17:13.380 --> 00:17:14.213
What does that look like?

00:17:14.213 --> 00:17:15.880
Are they still as active

00:17:15.880 --> 00:17:18.600
to do something that would be Hillary-like

00:17:18.600 --> 00:17:19.720
without a Hillary?

00:17:19.720 --> 00:17:21.030
That's what we're gonna find out.

00:17:21.030 --> 00:17:23.390
- So, basically you're
saying, if you're Trump,

00:17:23.390 --> 00:17:25.642
you're going, "Oh, please
let it be Bernie or Warren,"

00:17:25.642 --> 00:17:27.880
because you just think the
numbers-wise it's just like...

00:17:27.880 --> 00:17:28.733
- Or Biden.

00:17:29.760 --> 00:17:32.180
I throw him in there as the unelectable.

00:17:32.180 --> 00:17:34.260
So top three are, by far,

00:17:34.260 --> 00:17:36.763
the most unelectable in
the general election.

00:17:37.763 --> 00:17:39.000
- Why Biden?

00:17:39.000 --> 00:17:40.960
I get you on the Bernie, Elizabeth thing.

00:17:40.960 --> 00:17:42.567
If people have just had
it with the woke thing

00:17:42.567 --> 00:17:44.380
and the far left progressives,

00:17:44.380 --> 00:17:46.310
believe me, I hear you.

00:17:46.310 --> 00:17:48.210
But Biden at least seems like

00:17:48.210 --> 00:17:50.510
he would maybe bring some of the people

00:17:50.510 --> 00:17:52.150
that are just kind of fed up with Trump,

00:17:52.150 --> 00:17:53.560
keep some of the centrists.

00:17:53.560 --> 00:17:54.610
But you think then all the woke people

00:17:54.610 --> 00:17:56.630
would just try to take him
out or something like that?

00:17:56.630 --> 00:17:58.660
- Well, here's a question
I've been asking.

00:17:58.660 --> 00:18:00.647
I've been asking people, even in public,

00:18:00.647 --> 00:18:03.355
"Have you ever met a Biden supporter?"

00:18:03.355 --> 00:18:05.420
(Dave laughing)

00:18:05.420 --> 00:18:08.350
You're laughing because you
know you've never met one.

00:18:08.350 --> 00:18:10.120
I've met a Warren supporter, a Biden.

00:18:10.120 --> 00:18:11.210
You can go down the list.

00:18:11.210 --> 00:18:14.540
But Buttigieg, haven't
met any, or Klobuchar.

00:18:14.540 --> 00:18:16.810
But that's because she's low in the polls.

00:18:16.810 --> 00:18:18.830
But Biden's at the top of the polls.

00:18:18.830 --> 00:18:22.590
In fact, top of the polls even
in the general against Trump.

00:18:22.590 --> 00:18:24.580
And I've never met one?

00:18:24.580 --> 00:18:27.280
Or have I never met
one who would admit it?

00:18:27.280 --> 00:18:28.920
- Well, I guess there's
a certain set of people

00:18:28.920 --> 00:18:32.597
that are just deeply
unenthused who are just like,

00:18:32.597 --> 00:18:34.628
"Well, it's better than that,"

00:18:34.628 --> 00:18:36.960
so they don't run around with a Biden sign

00:18:36.960 --> 00:18:38.020
or something like that.

00:18:38.020 --> 00:18:41.360
- I have to think that
people who are supporting him

00:18:41.360 --> 00:18:43.290
don't know who else is in the race,

00:18:43.290 --> 00:18:46.913
and they also haven't seen
him on video recently.

00:18:48.030 --> 00:18:49.890
I don't wanna be unkind.

00:18:49.890 --> 00:18:51.720
When we first started seeing him

00:18:51.720 --> 00:18:53.770
I actually said in public,
"Let's be kind about this,

00:18:53.770 --> 00:18:56.350
"because this won't last long."

00:18:56.350 --> 00:18:58.370
The party will take
care of it on their own

00:18:58.370 --> 00:19:01.560
then we'll have a candidate
with full capability.

00:19:01.560 --> 00:19:04.210
But I feel, honestly,

00:19:04.210 --> 00:19:05.560
honestly, I feel bad for him.

00:19:05.560 --> 00:19:06.490
- [Dave] Yeah, I do too.

00:19:06.490 --> 00:19:07.723
- But at the same time,

00:19:08.572 --> 00:19:11.400
I don't feel like I can
pull a punch anymore

00:19:11.400 --> 00:19:12.910
if there's something
that needs to be said.

00:19:12.910 --> 00:19:14.567
In the beginning I was gonna say,

00:19:14.567 --> 00:19:16.117
"Just let this take care of itself.

00:19:16.117 --> 00:19:17.767
"We don't need to pile on."

00:19:17.767 --> 00:19:19.170
But now maybe we do.

00:19:19.170 --> 00:19:20.690
Maybe we do need to pile on

00:19:20.690 --> 00:19:22.044
if we see a real problem.

00:19:22.044 --> 00:19:23.540
I just don't think he's got

00:19:23.540 --> 00:19:26.080
the mental capacity,

00:19:26.080 --> 00:19:28.845
especially if you fast forward four years,

00:19:28.845 --> 00:19:31.780
or God help us, eight years.

00:19:31.780 --> 00:19:33.700
What would he be in eight years?

00:19:33.700 --> 00:19:35.350
- Well, it's ironic, because
I think a lot of people,

00:19:35.350 --> 00:19:37.700
and obviously you would
not be a Biden supporter,

00:19:37.700 --> 00:19:40.470
but you are trying to respectful of a guy

00:19:40.470 --> 00:19:41.840
just as a human being.

00:19:41.840 --> 00:19:45.013
But it does seem like
something is degrading there.

00:19:45.013 --> 00:19:47.160
It just does.
- He's not only a human being,

00:19:47.160 --> 00:19:49.310
but he's been a great
servant of the public.

00:19:49.310 --> 00:19:52.070
He's apparently well liked on both sides.

00:19:52.070 --> 00:19:54.350
I have a lot of good to say about him,

00:19:54.350 --> 00:19:55.700
but it's just not his time.

00:19:56.542 --> 00:19:59.800
- Do you think we're all
too focused on politics?

00:19:59.800 --> 00:20:01.640
As a guy that's not a pure political beast

00:20:01.640 --> 00:20:04.540
but is framing it for a lot of people.

00:20:04.540 --> 00:20:06.480
- I wonder how many are focused.

00:20:06.480 --> 00:20:08.220
You know, if you walked
down in the sidewalk

00:20:08.220 --> 00:20:11.487
and you sat down, "Name our few
people running for president

00:20:11.487 --> 00:20:12.960
"on the Democrat side,"

00:20:12.960 --> 00:20:15.080
how many could they even name?

00:20:15.080 --> 00:20:15.913
I don't know.

00:20:15.913 --> 00:20:17.733
Could they find Ukraine on
the map, to use that example?

00:20:17.733 --> 00:20:18.800
- [Dave] (chuckles) No.

00:20:18.800 --> 00:20:20.053
That I know they can't.

00:20:21.030 --> 00:20:22.756
- I feel like you and I,

00:20:22.756 --> 00:20:25.310
we're living in this Twitterverse

00:20:25.310 --> 00:20:27.160
where it seems like it's
the most important thing,

00:20:27.160 --> 00:20:28.750
but maybe it's not.

00:20:28.750 --> 00:20:31.060
- Does that, do you think,
sometimes make it a little harder

00:20:31.060 --> 00:20:33.563
to judge what's actually going on?

00:20:34.490 --> 00:20:37.360
Before 2016 it was like, because
we existed in that world,

00:20:37.360 --> 00:20:39.890
we could see something that
other people couldn't see.

00:20:39.890 --> 00:20:42.290
Now that world has sort of collided

00:20:42.290 --> 00:20:44.300
with mainstream in a bigger way

00:20:44.300 --> 00:20:47.050
so it does make it harder to
track some of these things.

00:20:47.050 --> 00:20:47.900
Does that make sense?

00:20:47.900 --> 00:20:49.940
- Well, I think it's
hard for all of us to see

00:20:49.940 --> 00:20:51.343
outside of our bubble.

00:20:52.528 --> 00:20:54.090
- That's what we're gonna talk about.

00:20:54.090 --> 00:20:57.700
- And so I don't imagine
that I'm free from that.

00:20:57.700 --> 00:20:59.510
The best I can do is

00:20:59.510 --> 00:21:01.450
use some technique to try to see

00:21:01.450 --> 00:21:03.550
the other levels as best I can.

00:21:03.550 --> 00:21:05.370
So putting the effort in
is the best you can do.

00:21:05.370 --> 00:21:07.950
You just can't tell if you're
seeing the whole field.

00:21:07.950 --> 00:21:09.550
That's the trouble.

00:21:09.550 --> 00:21:11.830
- Speaking of the bubble,
let's move it to the book.

00:21:11.830 --> 00:21:14.150
First off, the top title is "Loser Think".

00:21:14.150 --> 00:21:15.980
We'll get to the subtitle in just a sec.

00:21:15.980 --> 00:21:17.230
But when you're titling the book,

00:21:17.230 --> 00:21:20.287
were you afraid to go, "If I
call this thing 'Loser Think',

00:21:20.287 --> 00:21:21.380
"the average guy..."

00:21:21.380 --> 00:21:23.160
As you know because I
just wrote this thing,

00:21:23.160 --> 00:21:25.010
and we're dealing with
titles and all of this stuff.

00:21:25.010 --> 00:21:27.710
It's like you want them to
see it and be able to grab it.

00:21:27.710 --> 00:21:29.007
But did you think, "Uh oh.

00:21:29.007 --> 00:21:30.767
"If we put this out there
and it says 'Loser Think'

00:21:30.767 --> 00:21:31.600
"people are gonna be afraid

00:21:31.600 --> 00:21:33.087
"because they think if
they're gonna touch it

00:21:33.087 --> 00:21:34.950
"that means they're a loser,"
or something like that?

00:21:34.950 --> 00:21:36.884
What's the persuasion level
you were working with?

00:21:36.884 --> 00:21:38.880
- Yes, we thought about it.

00:21:38.880 --> 00:21:39.713
Here's...

00:21:39.713 --> 00:21:41.670
I'll just talk you through,
here's how we thought about it.

00:21:41.670 --> 00:21:44.440
Number one, I said, if
I give it this title

00:21:44.440 --> 00:21:46.997
will people ask me, "Why'd
you give it this title?

00:21:46.997 --> 00:21:48.305
"Isn't that a mistake?"

00:21:48.305 --> 00:21:50.160
(Dave laughing)
Bingo, bingo.

00:21:50.160 --> 00:21:51.190
Everybody asked me that.

00:21:51.190 --> 00:21:52.880
- I just fell right into the trap.

00:21:52.880 --> 00:21:54.540
- No, and that was anticipated

00:21:54.540 --> 00:21:56.200
that people would wonder that.

00:21:56.200 --> 00:21:59.730
Two, because there exists
the Dummies series of books

00:21:59.730 --> 00:22:01.240
and the Idiot's Guide,

00:22:01.240 --> 00:22:03.220
it's been proven that
people will buy a book

00:22:03.220 --> 00:22:04.670
that says they're an idiot on it.

00:22:04.670 --> 00:22:07.040
And lots of them.
(Dave laughing)

00:22:07.040 --> 00:22:08.269
These are two...

00:22:08.269 --> 00:22:11.090
The series, the Dummies
and the Idiot's Guide books

00:22:11.090 --> 00:22:14.047
are enormous, so clearly that.

00:22:14.047 --> 00:22:17.190
But here's the other insight
I had because of Dilbert.

00:22:17.190 --> 00:22:19.220
When I wrote Dilbert it was, of course,

00:22:19.220 --> 00:22:21.930
making fun of your boss, most of the time.

00:22:21.930 --> 00:22:24.510
But bosses kept buying my material.

00:22:24.510 --> 00:22:28.224
And I would be like, "Why are
you buying it," in my head.

00:22:28.224 --> 00:22:31.747
And they would say, "This
perfectly describes my bosses."

00:22:33.220 --> 00:22:34.839
- [Dave] Right, right,
right, even the bosses.

00:22:34.839 --> 00:22:35.672
- Nobody thought it was them.

00:22:35.672 --> 00:22:38.370
So we're primed to think
this is about someone else.

00:22:38.370 --> 00:22:41.290
I believe most people are
picking this up and saying

00:22:41.290 --> 00:22:42.230
one of two things.

00:22:42.230 --> 00:22:44.107
They're saying, "Ha ha, this will be fun.

00:22:44.107 --> 00:22:45.780
"We'll mock all those people."

00:22:45.780 --> 00:22:46.863
And it does,

00:22:46.863 --> 00:22:48.710
if they wanna see

00:22:48.710 --> 00:22:50.680
unproductive thinking
techniques in the book.

00:22:50.680 --> 00:22:53.869
And two, this will be a perfect
gift for my brother-in-law,

00:22:53.869 --> 00:22:55.450
(both laughing)

00:22:55.450 --> 00:22:58.100
and it'll be a private
joke that that's the title.

00:22:58.100 --> 00:22:59.457
- Right, so, the subtitle is,

00:22:59.457 --> 00:23:03.640
"How Untrained Brains
Are Ruining America".

00:23:03.640 --> 00:23:05.120
Now that makes a lot of sense to me

00:23:05.120 --> 00:23:06.520
because that seems like pretty much

00:23:06.520 --> 00:23:08.550
everything that's
happening at every level.

00:23:08.550 --> 00:23:12.603
At academia, at the political
level, at the media level.

00:23:13.560 --> 00:23:17.600
Do you mean it at every
level, just across the board?

00:23:17.600 --> 00:23:19.800
- Well, it's certainly
making everything worse.

00:23:19.800 --> 00:23:21.870
And the problem is that everybody thinks

00:23:21.870 --> 00:23:24.180
they have this thing called common sense.

00:23:24.180 --> 00:23:25.970
And therefore they think they're arguing

00:23:25.970 --> 00:23:28.650
about politics or priorities or something.

00:23:28.650 --> 00:23:30.250
And often, they're not.

00:23:30.250 --> 00:23:32.330
They're just unproductively thinking

00:23:32.330 --> 00:23:34.970
so that the thing they're
saying is actually nonsense.

00:23:34.970 --> 00:23:36.880
It's not an opinion, it's not a priority.

00:23:36.880 --> 00:23:38.430
It's just sort of nonsense

00:23:38.430 --> 00:23:40.430
because they don't know
how to productively think.

00:23:40.430 --> 00:23:43.030
So I thought, "Well, I'll
try to make it accessible."

00:23:43.030 --> 00:23:46.310
Instead of writing a how to
do logic book kinda book,

00:23:46.310 --> 00:23:47.860
which nobody's gonna read,

00:23:47.860 --> 00:23:51.460
I'll just give you the
friendly anecdote version

00:23:51.460 --> 00:23:53.430
that you could absorb pretty easily.

00:23:53.430 --> 00:23:55.760
- All right, so, if you're
trapped in your bubble

00:23:55.760 --> 00:23:58.740
and it's becoming a
self-fulfilling prophecy,

00:23:58.740 --> 00:24:02.010
what's the first step to
breaking out of your loser think?

00:24:02.010 --> 00:24:03.800
- Well, it's good to
know what all the various

00:24:03.800 --> 00:24:05.170
parts of loser think are.

00:24:05.170 --> 00:24:06.010
And I go through them.

00:24:06.010 --> 00:24:07.290
Like don't be a mind reader.

00:24:07.290 --> 00:24:09.260
Don't predict the future with analogies.

00:24:09.260 --> 00:24:10.550
History doesn't predict.

00:24:10.550 --> 00:24:12.700
Slippery slope isn't real.

00:24:12.700 --> 00:24:13.533
Fairness...

00:24:13.533 --> 00:24:15.440
- I wanna go through a few of those.

00:24:15.440 --> 00:24:17.590
- Without details, it's
good to know what those are.

00:24:17.590 --> 00:24:19.770
But beyond that, you should definitely

00:24:19.770 --> 00:24:22.120
be sampling both sides of the news.

00:24:22.120 --> 00:24:25.430
If you're not looking at Fox News and CNN,

00:24:25.430 --> 00:24:26.820
you don't know what's happening.

00:24:26.820 --> 00:24:28.700
Because I've tried doing that.

00:24:28.700 --> 00:24:31.530
Pick a topic, and before you've
heard what the other says,

00:24:31.530 --> 00:24:33.830
just consume one silo.

00:24:33.830 --> 00:24:35.210
And then you go to the other one.

00:24:35.210 --> 00:24:36.950
You're like, "What?

00:24:36.950 --> 00:24:38.977
"I didn't know anybody was saying this.

00:24:38.977 --> 00:24:40.810
"Are you telling me these facts are real?"

00:24:40.810 --> 00:24:42.520
And it doesn't matter
which way you're going.

00:24:42.520 --> 00:24:43.780
They're completely different.

00:24:43.780 --> 00:24:45.920
- But is it almost impossible
to break out of that?

00:24:45.920 --> 00:24:47.350
I mean, we all talk
about this all the time.

00:24:47.350 --> 00:24:48.630
We're catering news to ourselves.

00:24:48.630 --> 00:24:50.320
The algorithms are feeding us things

00:24:50.320 --> 00:24:53.170
that reinforce our thoughts already.

00:24:53.170 --> 00:24:54.513
But is it almost impossible
to break out of it

00:24:54.513 --> 00:24:56.740
because in a weird way, psychologically,

00:24:56.740 --> 00:24:57.920
we don't wanna break out of it?

00:24:57.920 --> 00:24:59.320
I know some people do,

00:24:59.320 --> 00:25:02.120
but that most of us, kinda, we just...

00:25:02.120 --> 00:25:03.720
Does it not sound corny?

00:25:03.720 --> 00:25:06.040
That's the safe space
we find, and we're good,

00:25:06.040 --> 00:25:07.930
and we're reinforced, and it's warm.

00:25:07.930 --> 00:25:10.100
- This is why I sneak in the back door.

00:25:10.100 --> 00:25:13.350
If I said to you, "Dave, I'm
gonna get you to change teams,"

00:25:13.350 --> 00:25:14.330
whatever your team is,

00:25:14.330 --> 00:25:16.380
Democrat, Republican, doesn't matter,

00:25:16.380 --> 00:25:18.664
you would immediately
say (imitates screeching)

00:25:18.664 --> 00:25:20.620
Or you'd be like, everything
you're shooting at me

00:25:20.620 --> 00:25:22.550
is bouncing off my force field.

00:25:22.550 --> 00:25:25.867
But if I say, "Here's
how you should think.

00:25:25.867 --> 00:25:27.617
"In any situation,

00:25:27.617 --> 00:25:30.120
"you wanna have something
to compare something to,"

00:25:30.120 --> 00:25:31.830
a very simple statement,

00:25:31.830 --> 00:25:32.753
people accept that

00:25:32.753 --> 00:25:36.170
because that's just
reasonable on its surface.

00:25:36.170 --> 00:25:37.260
And then you take that through,

00:25:37.260 --> 00:25:39.190
all right, now apply
this to your thinking.

00:25:39.190 --> 00:25:40.610
So you can sometimes get people

00:25:40.610 --> 00:25:43.150
to talk themselves out of their position

00:25:43.150 --> 00:25:44.420
by giving them better tools.

00:25:44.420 --> 00:25:45.517
But you can't just say,

00:25:45.517 --> 00:25:47.810
"Change teams because
you're on the wrong team."

00:25:47.810 --> 00:25:49.070
- How much of this do you think

00:25:49.070 --> 00:25:50.980
is just base human psychology?

00:25:50.980 --> 00:25:52.860
Just the stuff we're born with.

00:25:52.860 --> 00:25:55.910
That certain people are
just more programmed

00:25:55.910 --> 00:25:59.100
to go to just things that
reinforce their beliefs,

00:25:59.100 --> 00:26:00.440
and some people are just programmed

00:26:00.440 --> 00:26:03.110
to actually being a little bit more open?

00:26:03.110 --> 00:26:05.000
- Well, I think all of these things,

00:26:05.000 --> 00:26:06.470
you're born with a certain set

00:26:06.470 --> 00:26:08.500
of gifts and intelligence, et cetera.

00:26:08.500 --> 00:26:10.500
But in "Loser Think" I talk about how

00:26:11.840 --> 00:26:14.550
the experience you've had across domains

00:26:14.550 --> 00:26:16.770
is what gives you better vision.

00:26:16.770 --> 00:26:20.490
For example, if you'd never
been exposed to economics,

00:26:20.490 --> 00:26:22.310
I've got a degree in economics,

00:26:22.310 --> 00:26:25.030
you wouldn't know how economists think.

00:26:25.030 --> 00:26:28.370
So you might run into a
concept like a sunk cost.

00:26:28.370 --> 00:26:30.260
And if you'd never been exposed to this

00:26:30.260 --> 00:26:32.310
you might say, "Well,
I have common sense."

00:26:32.310 --> 00:26:34.697
And people would say, "We've
already spent all this money

00:26:34.697 --> 00:26:36.047
"so we better keep investing

00:26:36.047 --> 00:26:38.980
"because I don't wanna waste
that money I already spent."

00:26:38.980 --> 00:26:40.350
That's not sensible.

00:26:40.350 --> 00:26:41.677
An economist would say, "No.

00:26:41.677 --> 00:26:43.077
"The money that's gone

00:26:43.077 --> 00:26:45.117
"should not influence your next decision,

00:26:45.117 --> 00:26:46.570
"because it's gone."

00:26:46.570 --> 00:26:50.750
Now, the first time you hear
that, you say, "Oh, yeah."

00:26:50.750 --> 00:26:53.540
Like, you don't need to read a
book on it. You just hear it.

00:26:53.540 --> 00:26:55.760
And so I tried to make
it as simple as that.

00:26:55.760 --> 00:26:57.533
You just gotta hear it once, and you say,

00:26:57.533 --> 00:26:59.137
"Ah, yeah, that makes sense."

00:27:00.450 --> 00:27:03.600
- You think we all have
preprogrammed abilities

00:27:03.600 --> 00:27:05.610
to be flexible on some of these things,

00:27:05.610 --> 00:27:08.070
but you think that that can
pretty much always be...

00:27:08.070 --> 00:27:09.020
- Different levels.

00:27:10.440 --> 00:27:13.150
I mean, people are more or less locked in,

00:27:13.150 --> 00:27:14.590
more or less team players,

00:27:14.590 --> 00:27:17.705
more or less capable of looking at details

00:27:17.705 --> 00:27:19.260
and thinking properly.

00:27:19.260 --> 00:27:20.960
But everybody can do it better.

00:27:20.960 --> 00:27:22.480
- Do you think the
internet's changing that,

00:27:22.480 --> 00:27:25.270
just relative to the speed
that we get information,

00:27:25.270 --> 00:27:28.870
and the fact that you can
now go out there and find

00:27:28.870 --> 00:27:31.830
roughly a gajillion voices in two minutes

00:27:31.830 --> 00:27:34.120
that might be able to help
break some of your stuff?

00:27:34.120 --> 00:27:36.147
- I'll tell you, the
biggest problem, I think,

00:27:36.147 --> 00:27:38.500
and this is a brand new thought,

00:27:38.500 --> 00:27:41.370
is that we are, as humans, we're copiers,

00:27:41.370 --> 00:27:42.550
we're imitators.

00:27:42.550 --> 00:27:44.270
So if you wade into the internet,

00:27:44.270 --> 00:27:46.930
what kind of thinking
style are you gonna see?

00:27:46.930 --> 00:27:48.070
The worst.

00:27:48.070 --> 00:27:49.260
And people will imitate it.

00:27:49.260 --> 00:27:52.460
So they'll say, for example,
"Hey, your side did X."

00:27:52.460 --> 00:27:54.560
And what's the most common response?

00:27:54.560 --> 00:27:55.883
Your side did X.

00:27:56.830 --> 00:27:58.070
What did that help?

00:27:58.070 --> 00:27:58.950
That helped nothing.

00:27:58.950 --> 00:28:00.700
That wasn't even on the topic.

00:28:00.700 --> 00:28:02.101
You didn't even talk about your topic.

00:28:02.101 --> 00:28:04.330
- But if they're gonna
do it, we have to do it.

00:28:04.330 --> 00:28:07.420
- So some of it is, of
course, power, and influence,

00:28:07.420 --> 00:28:08.710
and persuasion and all that.

00:28:08.710 --> 00:28:10.569
Nobody's trying to think.

00:28:10.569 --> 00:28:11.980
You have to be able to weed out,

00:28:11.980 --> 00:28:14.110
okay, was anybody even trying,

00:28:14.110 --> 00:28:16.260
or were they just persuading?

00:28:16.260 --> 00:28:18.370
- Do you think most of us
just don't wanna try, though,

00:28:18.370 --> 00:28:19.203
at some level?

00:28:19.203 --> 00:28:20.750
I mean, most people just kinda...

00:28:20.750 --> 00:28:23.970
They don't wanna think
about things that seriously

00:28:23.970 --> 00:28:27.190
because that brings up a
lotta deep existential stuff.

00:28:27.190 --> 00:28:28.920
- Yeah, maybe, maybe.

00:28:28.920 --> 00:28:29.820
There are lots of reasons.

00:28:29.820 --> 00:28:30.820
We don't wanna be wrong.

00:28:30.820 --> 00:28:32.210
We don't like to be embarrassed.

00:28:32.210 --> 00:28:33.430
We don't like to change our mind.

00:28:33.430 --> 00:28:35.620
We don't wanna disappoint the team.

00:28:35.620 --> 00:28:37.730
And we're influenced by what we see,

00:28:37.730 --> 00:28:38.987
so we just join in and say,

00:28:38.987 --> 00:28:40.227
"Well, that's what my team's doing.

00:28:40.227 --> 00:28:41.940
"I guess I'll do some of that."

00:28:41.940 --> 00:28:43.600
- You talk about

00:28:43.600 --> 00:28:46.830
some of the dumb ideas
that are floating around,

00:28:46.830 --> 00:28:49.120
that thus create our own bubbles.

00:28:49.120 --> 00:28:50.573
I wanted to go through a couple of them.

00:28:50.573 --> 00:28:52.360
Because we hear these all the time,

00:28:52.360 --> 00:28:53.500
and they sorta sound right

00:28:53.500 --> 00:28:56.070
but you don't quite think they are right.

00:28:56.070 --> 00:28:58.670
- We know when history will repeat itself,

00:28:58.670 --> 00:28:59.503
and when it won't.

00:28:59.503 --> 00:29:00.830
We hear this all the time.

00:29:00.830 --> 00:29:01.800
- History repeats.

00:29:01.800 --> 00:29:03.160
- Yes, history repeats itself.

00:29:03.160 --> 00:29:06.630
- And because it has a phrase,
and we've heard the phrase,

00:29:06.630 --> 00:29:07.730
we give it weight.

00:29:07.730 --> 00:29:09.907
Because the brain will
say, "Well, it's a saying.

00:29:09.907 --> 00:29:13.117
"I'm pretty sure I've seen
that on a bumper sticker

00:29:13.117 --> 00:29:15.120
"so there must be something about that."

00:29:15.120 --> 00:29:17.000
But it's an illusion caused by the fact

00:29:17.000 --> 00:29:20.390
that we don't notice when
history doesn't repeat,

00:29:20.390 --> 00:29:21.790
which is most of the time.

00:29:21.790 --> 00:29:23.880
And why is it that history doesn't repeat?

00:29:23.880 --> 00:29:24.970
Because it can't.

00:29:24.970 --> 00:29:26.840
There's never two situations
that are the same.

00:29:26.840 --> 00:29:28.290
The variables are changed.

00:29:28.290 --> 00:29:30.080
And if nothing else has changed,

00:29:30.080 --> 00:29:32.730
you saw what went wrong the last time,

00:29:32.730 --> 00:29:34.930
so you fix it this time.

00:29:34.930 --> 00:29:37.670
It's a complete illusion that
we can predict the future

00:29:37.670 --> 00:29:40.100
because this time is like that last time.

00:29:40.100 --> 00:29:41.940
The most you can do is say

00:29:41.940 --> 00:29:46.080
people are still selfish,
stupid, and all those things.

00:29:46.080 --> 00:29:49.020
So if you put them in a
situation where they can steal,

00:29:49.020 --> 00:29:50.880
well, sure, they'll steal.

00:29:50.880 --> 00:29:51.713
And not everybody,

00:29:51.713 --> 00:29:54.290
but if you have enough people
introduced to a situation

00:29:54.290 --> 00:29:55.880
where you can steal something,

00:29:55.880 --> 00:29:57.220
somebody's gonna take a run at it.

00:29:57.220 --> 00:29:58.720
But that's not really history repeating.

00:29:58.720 --> 00:29:59.950
- Right, so you would say

00:29:59.950 --> 00:30:01.290
a better way to say it
would be something like,

00:30:01.290 --> 00:30:02.800
themes repeat themselves,

00:30:02.800 --> 00:30:04.860
just because it's based in our nature,

00:30:04.860 --> 00:30:06.160
not the specific event?

00:30:06.160 --> 00:30:07.790
- I would even make it smaller than that.

00:30:07.790 --> 00:30:09.830
I would say people are people.

00:30:09.830 --> 00:30:11.910
You know they're gonna be selfish

00:30:11.910 --> 00:30:14.280
and all the qualities of people,

00:30:14.280 --> 00:30:17.140
so any situation you put them in

00:30:17.140 --> 00:30:19.750
you should say, "Well, let's
look at cause and effect."

00:30:19.750 --> 00:30:22.373
If I put that person
where the free money is,

00:30:23.990 --> 00:30:25.530
I should expect money to be taken,

00:30:25.530 --> 00:30:27.840
but not because history repeats.

00:30:27.840 --> 00:30:29.450
There's just a simple cause and effect.

00:30:29.450 --> 00:30:31.180
- So if it doesn't repeat itself,

00:30:31.180 --> 00:30:33.260
what would you say to all
the history majors right now

00:30:33.260 --> 00:30:35.337
that are listening to this?

00:30:35.337 --> 00:30:37.850
What would be the point
of being a historian

00:30:37.850 --> 00:30:40.590
if not to have some sort
of roadmap for the future?

00:30:40.590 --> 00:30:42.600
- Well, indeed, history is what

00:30:42.600 --> 00:30:44.950
prevents it from repeating, in some sense,

00:30:44.950 --> 00:30:46.740
because you know what didn't work.

00:30:46.740 --> 00:30:50.480
So you might see history
repeat when it works.

00:30:50.480 --> 00:30:51.660
If something worked before

00:30:51.660 --> 00:30:53.880
you might see somebody
try to try it again.

00:30:53.880 --> 00:30:56.870
But even you take
anything, like North Korea.

00:30:56.870 --> 00:30:58.400
What was the main thing people said

00:30:58.400 --> 00:30:59.950
when President Trump got elected?

00:30:59.950 --> 00:31:02.087
It was, "Well, talking to North Korea

00:31:02.087 --> 00:31:03.730
"will never work," blah blah blah.

00:31:03.730 --> 00:31:04.920
And maybe it won't.

00:31:04.920 --> 00:31:06.760
But he got it further than anybody else.

00:31:06.760 --> 00:31:08.540
He got it into a personal level.

00:31:08.540 --> 00:31:10.770
It wasn't anything like history.

00:31:10.770 --> 00:31:13.370
So no matter how many
times history had repeated,

00:31:13.370 --> 00:31:16.080
you introduce this whole new
variable of President Trump

00:31:16.080 --> 00:31:18.510
and then history doesn't count anymore.

00:31:18.510 --> 00:31:19.700
- What do you make of that concept,

00:31:19.700 --> 00:31:22.620
that people who said
one thing two years ago

00:31:22.620 --> 00:31:23.940
say the complete opposite?

00:31:23.940 --> 00:31:27.110
I don't mean that they've
flipped all of their beliefs,

00:31:27.110 --> 00:31:29.050
but that it's always a team thing.

00:31:29.050 --> 00:31:32.100
It's like, if Obama had talked to...

00:31:32.100 --> 00:31:33.970
Gone to North Korea and
talked to Kim Jong-un,

00:31:33.970 --> 00:31:36.620
a certain set of people, the
Republicans in this case,

00:31:36.620 --> 00:31:37.453
would have been like, "He's crazy.

00:31:37.453 --> 00:31:38.450
"We can't talk to these people."

00:31:38.450 --> 00:31:40.100
If Trump does it, we can do it.

00:31:40.100 --> 00:31:41.700
You can flip that the other way.

00:31:42.535 --> 00:31:43.368
I guess that goes to what you're saying

00:31:43.368 --> 00:31:44.573
about the team thing.

00:31:44.573 --> 00:31:46.380
But do you think people realize

00:31:46.380 --> 00:31:47.720
the sort of absurdity?

00:31:47.720 --> 00:31:49.810
I'm not talking about
evolving over a period of time

00:31:49.810 --> 00:31:51.470
with a certain set of opinions.

00:31:51.470 --> 00:31:53.720
I'm talking about because
the players are different

00:31:53.720 --> 00:31:55.700
that you just completely
take an opposite side.

00:31:55.700 --> 00:31:56.960
- One of the great mysteries is

00:31:56.960 --> 00:31:58.460
you watch somebody who
doesn't agree with you,

00:31:58.460 --> 00:32:01.840
and they're doing that crazy
thinking like you mentioned.

00:32:01.840 --> 00:32:03.717
And you watch them, and
you think to yourself,

00:32:03.717 --> 00:32:06.037
"Is that real?
(Dave laughing)

00:32:06.037 --> 00:32:08.100
"Do they really think that?"

00:32:08.100 --> 00:32:09.520
And I spend time doing that too.

00:32:09.520 --> 00:32:11.960
But one of the advantages I have is,

00:32:11.960 --> 00:32:13.340
having studied hypnosis,

00:32:13.340 --> 00:32:16.210
I understand people to be
irrational most of the time.

00:32:16.210 --> 00:32:19.000
And I understand them to be rationalizers,

00:32:19.000 --> 00:32:22.860
and I understand confirmation
bias and cognitive dissonance.

00:32:22.860 --> 00:32:24.620
So my take is that most of the time

00:32:24.620 --> 00:32:27.490
they actually believe what they're saying,

00:32:27.490 --> 00:32:29.280
because they can't see it.

00:32:29.280 --> 00:32:30.423
They're the ones who are blinded to it

00:32:30.423 --> 00:32:33.050
so that it looks like
it makes sense to them.

00:32:33.050 --> 00:32:34.560
- Do you think there's some reason,

00:32:34.560 --> 00:32:36.700
like an obvious psychological reason

00:32:36.700 --> 00:32:39.600
that the people who seem most obsessed

00:32:39.600 --> 00:32:41.650
by politics on a day-to-day basis,

00:32:41.650 --> 00:32:44.290
and I'm not talking
the broad-themed stuff,

00:32:44.290 --> 00:32:45.123
but as you said before,

00:32:45.123 --> 00:32:47.980
you're really uninterested in politics.

00:32:47.980 --> 00:32:50.633
But the people that are obsessed
with the nitty gritty...

00:32:50.633 --> 00:32:51.470
(clears throat) Excuse me.

00:32:51.470 --> 00:32:52.980
The nitty gritty what happened today,

00:32:52.980 --> 00:32:55.630
what happened tomorrow, or
what's happening tomorrow,

00:32:55.630 --> 00:32:57.540
there's a reason that
they're so miserable.

00:32:57.540 --> 00:32:58.870
Because it's sort of...

00:32:58.870 --> 00:33:00.470
It's like an untenable system

00:33:00.470 --> 00:33:02.220
that they're playing with all the time.

00:33:02.220 --> 00:33:03.780
- Well, you know,

00:33:03.780 --> 00:33:06.330
people are different so it's
probably all different reasons.

00:33:06.330 --> 00:33:07.690
I do it for entertainment.

00:33:07.690 --> 00:33:10.570
And I think other people
are drawn in for the fight.

00:33:10.570 --> 00:33:12.053
They like the fight.

00:33:12.920 --> 00:33:13.980
What's my fight today?

00:33:13.980 --> 00:33:15.430
Ooh, there's a new headline.

00:33:15.430 --> 00:33:17.090
Let me get into that fight.

00:33:17.090 --> 00:33:18.970
There's definitely a, I don't know,

00:33:18.970 --> 00:33:22.220
dopamine or serotonin hit that people get

00:33:22.220 --> 00:33:23.490
just from making a good point.

00:33:23.490 --> 00:33:24.873
It's like, "Ah, got that guy

00:33:24.873 --> 00:33:26.912
"who said something bad
about this celebrity.

00:33:26.912 --> 00:33:28.208
"Ah, got him."

00:33:28.208 --> 00:33:29.041
- Got him.

00:33:29.041 --> 00:33:30.440
Somehow I think if this was a real fight,

00:33:30.440 --> 00:33:32.810
like involving weapons, they
would be much more reserved.

00:33:32.810 --> 00:33:34.183
What do you think?
- I know I would.

00:33:34.183 --> 00:33:36.330
(both chuckling)
- Yeah, exactly.

00:33:36.330 --> 00:33:38.510
Okay, let's plow through
a couple of these others.

00:33:38.510 --> 00:33:39.343
This is a good one

00:33:39.343 --> 00:33:41.870
because we hear some version
of this all the time online.

00:33:41.870 --> 00:33:43.160
We can tell the difference

00:33:43.160 --> 00:33:46.730
between evidence and coincidences.

00:33:46.730 --> 00:33:47.563
- Yeah.

00:33:48.800 --> 00:33:52.530
We go through our life
thinking that things matter.

00:33:52.530 --> 00:33:53.640
Let me give a perfect example.

00:33:53.640 --> 00:33:55.310
If you were a fiction writer

00:33:55.310 --> 00:33:57.730
and you were writing a book,
or even reading a book,

00:33:57.730 --> 00:34:01.560
and somebody put on a watch,

00:34:01.560 --> 00:34:04.500
you'd say to yourself,
"Oh, later, that watch..."

00:34:04.500 --> 00:34:05.600
- [Dave] Something's
going on with the watch.

00:34:05.600 --> 00:34:06.860
- Pay attention to the watch

00:34:06.860 --> 00:34:09.460
because the author
included that for a reason.

00:34:09.460 --> 00:34:12.670
So it's easy for some people
to have some experiences

00:34:12.670 --> 00:34:14.220
to imagine everything's connected.

00:34:14.220 --> 00:34:16.060
Musicians, poets, artists,

00:34:16.060 --> 00:34:17.740
they imagine a connected world

00:34:17.740 --> 00:34:19.300
because that's what they live in.

00:34:19.300 --> 00:34:22.820
Whereas the economist,
the scientist, the lawyer,

00:34:22.820 --> 00:34:24.810
if they're not advocating,

00:34:24.810 --> 00:34:26.527
would say, "Oh, you've
gotta look at these.

00:34:26.527 --> 00:34:29.457
"You've gotta look at all
these coincidence individually

00:34:29.457 --> 00:34:32.191
"and say, 'All right, is this
real, or just a coincidence?'"

00:34:32.191 --> 00:34:36.440
We're creatures who think
coincidence matters.

00:34:36.440 --> 00:34:39.300
And if we don't really police ourselves

00:34:39.300 --> 00:34:41.000
we create a bubble by imagining

00:34:41.000 --> 00:34:43.160
our little coincidences are meaningful.

00:34:43.160 --> 00:34:44.970
I give one example in the book.

00:34:44.970 --> 00:34:46.050
I was walking home one day,

00:34:46.050 --> 00:34:48.280
and I had bought some Sharpies,

00:34:48.280 --> 00:34:50.320
because I like them for
a variety of reasons.

00:34:50.320 --> 00:34:53.880
I took it out of my bag,
literally was holding in my hand,

00:34:53.880 --> 00:34:58.170
and Fox News was on, and Greg
Gutfeld was on, on his show.

00:34:58.170 --> 00:34:59.817
And he says, "I love Sharpies.

00:34:59.817 --> 00:35:00.650
"I really want Sharpies."

00:35:00.650 --> 00:35:02.590
I'm like, seriously?

00:35:02.590 --> 00:35:04.610
While it's in my hand.

00:35:04.610 --> 00:35:06.588
And how many times have
you seen a TV show...

00:35:06.588 --> 00:35:09.138
- But Scott, there are no
coincidence, I thought?

00:35:09.138 --> 00:35:09.971
What could this mean?

00:35:09.971 --> 00:35:11.880
So you're saying that's not
the universe talking to you.

00:35:11.880 --> 00:35:13.200
Because we've all had that moment.

00:35:13.200 --> 00:35:15.700
And you go, "Whoa, that has some meaning."

00:35:15.700 --> 00:35:17.320
But the way, I've been on
Gutfeld's show many times.

00:35:17.320 --> 00:35:18.560
He loves Sharpies.

00:35:18.560 --> 00:35:19.393
- He does.

00:35:19.393 --> 00:35:21.070
- He's got a whole thing of Sharpies.

00:35:21.070 --> 00:35:22.490
There's Sharpies in all his drawers.

00:35:22.490 --> 00:35:23.627
They're just filled up with Sharpies.

00:35:23.627 --> 00:35:24.930
- He and I could have a club

00:35:24.930 --> 00:35:26.500
of just people who love Sharpies.

00:35:26.500 --> 00:35:27.990
- But do you think truly that there's...

00:35:27.990 --> 00:35:29.610
Do you think there's no chance

00:35:29.610 --> 00:35:31.840
that there's meaning
in that sort of thing?

00:35:31.840 --> 00:35:33.440
Like, I know the Sharpie
example is kind of simple.

00:35:33.440 --> 00:35:36.390
But, you know, you're having
some deep, deep thought,

00:35:36.390 --> 00:35:37.730
and you're watching TV

00:35:37.730 --> 00:35:39.050
and then suddenly a character

00:35:39.050 --> 00:35:42.291
says exactly what you're
thinking. Now, I get it, I get it.

00:35:42.291 --> 00:35:44.350
You're just talking about
sort of law of averages also.

00:35:44.350 --> 00:35:45.890
Like, every now and again
this is gonna happen.

00:35:45.890 --> 00:35:47.900
You're never gonna notice the
ones when it doesn't happen,

00:35:47.900 --> 00:35:49.080
which is all day long.

00:35:49.080 --> 00:35:49.913
I get that.

00:35:49.913 --> 00:35:54.760
But do you think that there's
no meaning or value to that?

00:35:54.760 --> 00:35:57.100
- Well, not the examples that we gave.

00:35:57.100 --> 00:35:58.490
There are no meanings to those,

00:35:58.490 --> 00:36:01.530
unless we're a simulation,
and we're software,

00:36:01.530 --> 00:36:03.730
and there's a little code reuse going on.

00:36:03.730 --> 00:36:05.040
- Maybe we'll end with that.
- Can't rule that out.

00:36:05.040 --> 00:36:06.330
- Let's end with the simulation stuff.

00:36:06.330 --> 00:36:07.750
Let's stay with the book for now,

00:36:07.750 --> 00:36:09.820
but we'll go to simulation at the end.

00:36:09.820 --> 00:36:11.010
- Short of that,

00:36:11.010 --> 00:36:14.870
it can mean something
in a criminal situation.

00:36:14.870 --> 00:36:16.550
If somebody gets killed,

00:36:16.550 --> 00:36:19.641
and their spouse has booked
a flight out that morning,

00:36:19.641 --> 00:36:20.960
(both chuckling)

00:36:20.960 --> 00:36:22.310
and they didn't pack a bag,

00:36:23.670 --> 00:36:25.620
maybe that means something.

00:36:25.620 --> 00:36:27.810
So certainly in a political context...

00:36:27.810 --> 00:36:30.710
I'm sorry, the legal context,
you have to track them down.

00:36:31.830 --> 00:36:34.110
- What about just in a personal context?

00:36:34.110 --> 00:36:36.140
Like if you find meaning in something

00:36:36.140 --> 00:36:38.780
that may be purely coincidental,

00:36:38.780 --> 00:36:41.080
or however else you wanna describe it,

00:36:41.080 --> 00:36:42.280
ridiculous, or whatever.

00:36:42.280 --> 00:36:45.390
But if you personally
find some meaning in it.

00:36:45.390 --> 00:36:46.940
- Here's my advice.

00:36:46.940 --> 00:36:49.850
We're bombarded with
coincidences all the time.

00:36:49.850 --> 00:36:52.027
When they work in your
favor you should say that,

00:36:52.027 --> 00:36:53.617
"Well, that means something.

00:36:53.617 --> 00:36:55.667
"That means I should write this book.

00:36:55.667 --> 00:36:58.780
"That's telling me that I'm
gonna get a check in the mail."

00:36:58.780 --> 00:37:00.520
And if it looks a coincidence

00:37:00.520 --> 00:37:01.970
that's telling you
something bad's gonna happen

00:37:01.970 --> 00:37:04.320
you say, "Pfft, coincidence."

00:37:04.320 --> 00:37:06.200
- All right, seems fair enough.

00:37:06.200 --> 00:37:09.053
The simplest explanation is usually true.

00:37:10.540 --> 00:37:12.467
- People like to win arguments by saying,

00:37:12.467 --> 00:37:15.730
"Yours is complicated, but
mine's the simple one."

00:37:15.730 --> 00:37:17.630
And the problem is,

00:37:17.630 --> 00:37:20.123
first of all, the world
sometimes is complicated.

00:37:21.360 --> 00:37:25.640
Pick any major thing in the
news, it's pretty complicated.

00:37:25.640 --> 00:37:27.840
But the real problem is that we all think

00:37:27.840 --> 00:37:30.440
our explanation is the simple one.

00:37:30.440 --> 00:37:34.430
That's where the cognitive
problem comes in.

00:37:34.430 --> 00:37:38.590
My example I use is, "How
did the world get here?"

00:37:38.590 --> 00:37:40.510
Well, ask the creationist.

00:37:40.510 --> 00:37:41.343
God did it.

00:37:41.343 --> 00:37:43.280
You can't get any simpler than that.

00:37:43.280 --> 00:37:44.470
God did it.

00:37:44.470 --> 00:37:47.367
Ask the scientist who's the evolutionist,

00:37:47.367 --> 00:37:48.720
"How did it all get here?"

00:37:48.720 --> 00:37:50.150
Gradual change.

00:37:50.150 --> 00:37:51.810
Big Bang, simple.

00:37:51.810 --> 00:37:53.260
Which one is the simple one?

00:37:53.260 --> 00:37:55.840
Well, everybody thinks their
explanation is the simple one.

00:37:55.840 --> 00:37:57.880
Especially the one-variable people

00:37:57.880 --> 00:38:00.077
who will look at a
complicated thing and say,

00:38:00.077 --> 00:38:01.717
"Well, one variable, got it.

00:38:01.717 --> 00:38:04.010
"Simple, it's that one variable."

00:38:04.010 --> 00:38:05.763
- So which was it, God or Big Bang?

00:38:06.900 --> 00:38:08.680
- Simulation.
(both laughing)

00:38:08.680 --> 00:38:09.570
- Again, we're gonna...

00:38:09.570 --> 00:38:10.403
I do wanna do...

00:38:10.403 --> 00:38:12.550
- Try to keep that away from
me. You can't, you can't.

00:38:12.550 --> 00:38:14.990
- We will end with simulation then.

00:38:14.990 --> 00:38:17.260
But sometimes we need some simple answers

00:38:17.260 --> 00:38:19.540
in a complex world, do you think?

00:38:19.540 --> 00:38:22.820
- Well, it helps if you can simplify,

00:38:22.820 --> 00:38:24.470
but you don't wanna oversimplify.

00:38:24.470 --> 00:38:26.030
And then how do you know, how do you know?

00:38:26.030 --> 00:38:28.230
So every situation is
a little bit different.

00:38:28.230 --> 00:38:29.500
But certainly, if you're looking at,

00:38:29.500 --> 00:38:30.670
let's say, climate change,

00:38:30.670 --> 00:38:32.810
I don't wanna get into that in any detail,

00:38:32.810 --> 00:38:33.973
but to just say...

00:38:36.520 --> 00:38:39.550
I'm shaking my head because I
can barely say this out loud.

00:38:39.550 --> 00:38:42.680
One of the things I hear
the most from the skeptics

00:38:42.680 --> 00:38:47.680
is that the scientists forgot
to account for the sun.

00:38:47.837 --> 00:38:49.370
(chuckles) The sun.

00:38:49.370 --> 00:38:51.750
- Wait, they claim that the
scientists forgot about the sun?

00:38:51.750 --> 00:38:54.060
- That they didn't
account for solar flares

00:38:54.060 --> 00:38:55.560
or solar activity.

00:38:55.560 --> 00:38:58.220
And I always say the same
thing, which is, all right.

00:38:58.220 --> 00:39:01.350
It's your job to figure
out why the Earth is warm.

00:39:01.350 --> 00:39:02.460
You thought of the sun.

00:39:02.460 --> 00:39:03.490
- [Dave] The sun might be in there.

00:39:03.490 --> 00:39:04.630
- You might have gotten something wrong,

00:39:04.630 --> 00:39:06.830
but it wasn't because
you didn't look at it.

00:39:06.830 --> 00:39:08.200
You thought of the sun.

00:39:08.200 --> 00:39:10.150
So come up with a better skepticism.

00:39:10.150 --> 00:39:11.940
Skepticism is fine.

00:39:11.940 --> 00:39:13.627
But if you simplify it into,

00:39:13.627 --> 00:39:15.700
"I think they forgot to look at the sun,"

00:39:15.700 --> 00:39:17.130
you're not helping.

00:39:17.130 --> 00:39:18.670
- It's funny you bring up
climate change, though.

00:39:18.670 --> 00:39:20.230
Without going too far
down that rabbit hole,

00:39:20.230 --> 00:39:24.160
it reminds me of something
like the Paris Accords,

00:39:24.160 --> 00:39:25.150
first when they got signed

00:39:25.150 --> 00:39:27.300
and then when they got retracted,

00:39:27.300 --> 00:39:28.310
or whatever you wanna call it, by Trump.

00:39:28.310 --> 00:39:30.970
It was like, all of these
people, it all sounds good.

00:39:30.970 --> 00:39:32.440
We're gonna cut these emissions.

00:39:32.440 --> 00:39:34.350
We're gonna do this, we'll pay for this.

00:39:34.350 --> 00:39:36.710
It all sounds good in a simple sense,

00:39:36.710 --> 00:39:37.800
if you don't think about it.

00:39:37.800 --> 00:39:40.070
And then we are now out of the Accords.

00:39:40.070 --> 00:39:42.110
From what I understand,
we're actually exceeding

00:39:42.110 --> 00:39:45.760
many of the limitations
that were originally signed.

00:39:45.760 --> 00:39:47.450
And there was no mechanism

00:39:47.450 --> 00:39:49.420
to force anyone to actually do anything.

00:39:49.420 --> 00:39:51.360
It was literally just a piece of paper.

00:39:51.360 --> 00:39:53.890
But all these people
were screaming about it

00:39:53.890 --> 00:39:54.950
like it was the end of the world.

00:39:54.950 --> 00:39:56.790
And we see this with net neutrality.

00:39:56.790 --> 00:39:58.060
It was the end of the world.

00:39:58.060 --> 00:39:59.840
Is your internet still working?

00:39:59.840 --> 00:40:01.230
- Barely.
(Dave laughing)

00:40:01.230 --> 00:40:03.020
- Oh, right, you had a
Wi-Fi problem this morning.

00:40:03.020 --> 00:40:06.580
But that, basically, we
go from end to the world,

00:40:06.580 --> 00:40:09.440
to end of the world, to end
of the world situations.

00:40:09.440 --> 00:40:10.424
- Yeah.

00:40:10.424 --> 00:40:12.770
We're locked into whatever's
the scariest thing.

00:40:12.770 --> 00:40:13.880
What's the scariest thing?

00:40:13.880 --> 00:40:17.930
With the example of the
Paris Climate Accord,

00:40:17.930 --> 00:40:21.580
if you have, let's say, more
experience in this world,

00:40:21.580 --> 00:40:24.333
probably nothing gets
solved with a meeting.

00:40:24.333 --> 00:40:25.166
(both chuckling)

00:40:25.166 --> 00:40:27.530
And we had an agreement,
and then everybody kept it.

00:40:27.530 --> 00:40:29.450
That's not the real world.

00:40:29.450 --> 00:40:30.710
And I think--

00:40:30.710 --> 00:40:32.880
- But that just makes a lot
of people feel good, right?

00:40:32.880 --> 00:40:34.630
When I've mentioned this

00:40:34.630 --> 00:40:36.490
and then people confront me about it,

00:40:36.490 --> 00:40:38.180
and then I say to them,
"Well, what was in it,"

00:40:38.180 --> 00:40:41.270
or was any country, actually,

00:40:41.270 --> 00:40:44.250
was there any mechanism to
force countries to abide by it?

00:40:44.250 --> 00:40:45.920
Or what has happened since we got out?

00:40:45.920 --> 00:40:47.430
I mean, just basic questions.

00:40:47.430 --> 00:40:49.470
And I'm not saying I'm an
expert in this by any stretch.

00:40:49.470 --> 00:40:52.130
But just asking these simple questions.

00:40:52.130 --> 00:40:54.130
People that are screaming about it

00:40:54.130 --> 00:40:56.230
suddenly admit they know nothing about it.

00:40:56.230 --> 00:40:57.063
(Scott chuckles)

00:40:57.063 --> 00:40:59.810
- I think they trusted
their side too much,

00:40:59.810 --> 00:41:01.710
because they've heard it's important.

00:41:01.710 --> 00:41:03.300
They hear it on the news all the time.

00:41:03.300 --> 00:41:04.400
Well, it must be important.

00:41:04.400 --> 00:41:05.650
Do I need to know the details?

00:41:05.650 --> 00:41:07.770
All the smart people
just said it's important.

00:41:07.770 --> 00:41:10.760
Generals and politicians, senators.

00:41:10.760 --> 00:41:12.560
If they're all saying it's important,

00:41:12.560 --> 00:41:15.530
and you're stuck in your silo
and that's all you're hearing,

00:41:15.530 --> 00:41:17.410
it's reasonable to think it is

00:41:17.410 --> 00:41:19.110
without you knowing all the details.

00:41:19.110 --> 00:41:22.280
But like you said, you can
uncover that flaw so easily.

00:41:22.280 --> 00:41:23.290
- What do you do with the people

00:41:23.290 --> 00:41:26.070
once you've uncovered the flaw,

00:41:26.070 --> 00:41:28.080
and now they're kinda freaking out?

00:41:28.080 --> 00:41:30.230
Because they don't want
that to happen, right?

00:41:30.230 --> 00:41:31.350
That's kinda scary.

00:41:31.350 --> 00:41:33.740
- Well, I do the method
that you just described.

00:41:33.740 --> 00:41:35.460
You do it in question form.

00:41:35.460 --> 00:41:38.140
If you do it in a statement
form, it bounces off.

00:41:38.140 --> 00:41:40.090
If you do it in question form it's like,

00:41:40.090 --> 00:41:42.620
what was it about the climate agreement,

00:41:42.620 --> 00:41:44.330
the Paris Climate Agreement,

00:41:44.330 --> 00:41:45.750
that you thought was the good part?

00:41:45.750 --> 00:41:48.260
What was the part that was
really gonna make a difference?

00:41:48.260 --> 00:41:50.040
How would that affect China

00:41:50.040 --> 00:41:51.980
versus how it'd affect the United States?

00:41:51.980 --> 00:41:53.910
What would it do to employment?

00:41:53.910 --> 00:41:55.810
What would it do to our economy?

00:41:55.810 --> 00:41:57.580
So I just ask the questions

00:41:57.580 --> 00:41:59.440
and let people talk themselves out of it.

00:41:59.440 --> 00:42:03.270
You could affect people's
confidence in the short term,

00:42:03.270 --> 00:42:04.420
and then they have to go back

00:42:04.420 --> 00:42:06.090
and re-engineer their thoughts.

00:42:06.090 --> 00:42:08.160
And sometimes they might change.

00:42:08.160 --> 00:42:09.040
- Sometimes.
- Sometimes.

00:42:09.040 --> 00:42:10.480
- Not always, definitely not always.

00:42:10.480 --> 00:42:11.313
You'll appreciate this.

00:42:11.313 --> 00:42:13.720
A couple weeks ago I was having a debate.

00:42:13.720 --> 00:42:15.710
I was having conversation
with an old friend

00:42:15.710 --> 00:42:17.740
about the trans issue.

00:42:17.740 --> 00:42:20.680
And I said I'm completely
fine with trans people.

00:42:20.680 --> 00:42:22.840
Obviously I want everyone
to be treated equally,

00:42:22.840 --> 00:42:24.940
and with the same laws, and with respect,

00:42:24.940 --> 00:42:25.773
and all of those things.

00:42:25.773 --> 00:42:27.140
I said, all I'm saying is

00:42:27.140 --> 00:42:29.300
that we can't say that
biology doesn't exist,

00:42:29.300 --> 00:42:30.760
that there's a difference
between male and female.

00:42:30.760 --> 00:42:32.010
That's all I said.

00:42:32.010 --> 00:42:32.967
And he said to me,

00:42:32.967 --> 00:42:36.653
"Dave, you're not thinking
with quantum physics."

00:42:36.653 --> 00:42:37.570
(Scott chuckling)

00:42:37.570 --> 00:42:40.217
And I said, "Could you
tell me a little bit

00:42:40.217 --> 00:42:42.270
"about how quantum physics
is related to this?"

00:42:42.270 --> 00:42:44.523
And he goes, "Now you're
just trying to get me."

00:42:44.523 --> 00:42:46.210
(Scott laughing)
And I thought that is just

00:42:46.210 --> 00:42:48.810
the perfect example of all of this.

00:42:48.810 --> 00:42:52.020
He just had this idea that quantum physics

00:42:52.020 --> 00:42:53.200
has something to do,

00:42:53.200 --> 00:42:55.870
if I could just think in some other realm,

00:42:55.870 --> 00:42:58.010
then somehow biology of this world,

00:42:58.010 --> 00:42:59.440
but actually have nothing to do anything.

00:42:59.440 --> 00:43:00.670
And when I asked him about it,

00:43:00.670 --> 00:43:01.503
he didn't know anything about it either.

00:43:01.503 --> 00:43:02.863
- You might be the best
debater of all time

00:43:02.863 --> 00:43:06.530
because you chased him into
another dimension of reality.

00:43:06.530 --> 00:43:08.167
He was like, "Oh, I'm out.

00:43:08.167 --> 00:43:09.344
"This reality is not serving me well."

00:43:09.344 --> 00:43:11.230
- And then he just disappeared.
He was just a smoke.

00:43:11.230 --> 00:43:13.700
- Just go on.
- And that was it.

00:43:13.700 --> 00:43:15.710
Stay in your channel.

00:43:15.710 --> 00:43:16.543
- Yeah.

00:43:16.543 --> 00:43:20.390
The worst advice you could
ever give or ever get

00:43:20.390 --> 00:43:21.910
is stick to your day job,

00:43:21.910 --> 00:43:23.480
stay in your channel,

00:43:23.480 --> 00:43:25.320
stay in your lane.

00:43:25.320 --> 00:43:26.620
I hear this all the time,

00:43:26.620 --> 00:43:29.110
especially when I
transitioned from cartooning,

00:43:29.110 --> 00:43:30.430
and even before that,

00:43:30.430 --> 00:43:31.580
when I tried to transition

00:43:31.580 --> 00:43:33.830
from my cubicle life to cartooning.

00:43:33.830 --> 00:43:35.720
Not a lot of support.

00:43:35.720 --> 00:43:38.670
Not a lot of people saw that
that would work out for me.

00:43:38.670 --> 00:43:41.920
But if you imagine what
would the world look like

00:43:41.920 --> 00:43:44.210
if everybody took that advice.

00:43:44.210 --> 00:43:46.330
Well, people making bicycles

00:43:46.330 --> 00:43:48.280
would never try to make an airplane,

00:43:48.280 --> 00:43:49.180
for one.

00:43:49.180 --> 00:43:50.360
Steve Jobs wouldn't be

00:43:50.360 --> 00:43:53.060
inventing any companies and computers.

00:43:53.060 --> 00:43:55.230
So you're already down the lane, the line,

00:43:55.230 --> 00:43:57.600
and people leaving their lane

00:43:57.600 --> 00:43:59.173
was all the good stuff.

00:44:00.150 --> 00:44:03.640
Now, I also tell people to
build their talent stacks,

00:44:03.640 --> 00:44:04.700
I call it.

00:44:04.700 --> 00:44:07.080
If you're good at this, or a few things,

00:44:07.080 --> 00:44:08.250
leave your lane a little bit

00:44:08.250 --> 00:44:09.830
and pick up a few other skills.

00:44:09.830 --> 00:44:11.810
You might get embarrassed,
you might get tired,

00:44:11.810 --> 00:44:12.980
but boy are you gonna be happy

00:44:12.980 --> 00:44:14.953
when you've got combined skills

00:44:14.953 --> 00:44:17.070
that are practically a superpower.

00:44:17.070 --> 00:44:19.100
- Is that another one of
those things where it just

00:44:19.100 --> 00:44:20.930
depends a little bit
about how you're wired?

00:44:20.930 --> 00:44:23.000
Like some people are
just all in on one thing

00:44:23.000 --> 00:44:25.180
and that is how they
can operate their best?

00:44:25.180 --> 00:44:28.370
And some people are a little
more designed that they can

00:44:28.370 --> 00:44:30.020
maybe change careers more easily,

00:44:30.020 --> 00:44:32.670
or just learn what their stack
is a little bit more easily,

00:44:32.670 --> 00:44:34.360
and some people just can't do it?

00:44:34.360 --> 00:44:36.730
Or are you saying that's
always your job to overcome it?

00:44:36.730 --> 00:44:39.580
- There's certainly a personal difference.

00:44:39.580 --> 00:44:41.770
There are some people who just wanna stay

00:44:41.770 --> 00:44:44.020
doing what they're doing,
and do the best they can.

00:44:44.020 --> 00:44:47.120
And if you're Tiger Woods,
that's a great idea.

00:44:47.120 --> 00:44:50.060
Tiger Woods doesn't also
need to know accounting.

00:44:50.060 --> 00:44:51.150
Well, except

00:44:51.150 --> 00:44:51.983
maybe it would help him.

00:44:51.983 --> 00:44:56.720
Somebody else is managing his
money and that's never safe.

00:44:56.720 --> 00:44:59.350
You can usually tell if
you're in that situation.

00:44:59.350 --> 00:45:03.320
Yeah, I need to just run on
this horse as long as I can.

00:45:03.320 --> 00:45:06.290
But for most of us, in
just ordinary life...

00:45:06.290 --> 00:45:09.820
I can only be a high-end cartoonist

00:45:09.820 --> 00:45:13.400
because I assembled a
bunch of pretty good skills

00:45:13.400 --> 00:45:14.850
that anybody could have done.

00:45:15.968 --> 00:45:19.640
I draw like a, you know, a
drunken monkey, I like to say.

00:45:19.640 --> 00:45:21.250
But it got better over time.

00:45:21.250 --> 00:45:23.920
I got better tools,
figured out how to do it.

00:45:23.920 --> 00:45:26.130
I didn't have any
experience writing humor,

00:45:26.130 --> 00:45:28.110
but I figured it out over time.

00:45:28.110 --> 00:45:30.130
And I had some business experience,

00:45:30.130 --> 00:45:34.213
so I had a canvas to write on.

00:45:35.350 --> 00:45:37.810
I'm a perfect example of
a bunch of mediocre skills

00:45:37.810 --> 00:45:40.120
that just work pretty well together.

00:45:40.120 --> 00:45:42.160
- I wanna back up to
something we started with here

00:45:42.160 --> 00:45:43.110
about your career,

00:45:43.110 --> 00:45:45.080
because it does fit "Loser Think".

00:45:45.080 --> 00:45:46.930
The idea that a guy who, I'm guessing,

00:45:46.930 --> 00:45:49.950
you were probably decent
at both of your day jobs

00:45:49.950 --> 00:45:51.100
when they told you...

00:45:51.100 --> 00:45:52.500
Halfway decent?

00:45:52.500 --> 00:45:55.420
Probably not totally in
dereliction of your duties.

00:45:55.420 --> 00:45:57.086
Fair to say?
- I showed up on time.

00:45:57.086 --> 00:45:57.919
- Okay, you showed up on time.

00:45:57.919 --> 00:45:59.960
So you were probably pretty
decent at what you did.

00:45:59.960 --> 00:46:02.340
The idea that they said, "Okay, well,"

00:46:02.340 --> 00:46:03.687
at two different jobs,

00:46:03.687 --> 00:46:07.637
"Diversity, you're in effect
a straight white male,

00:46:07.637 --> 00:46:09.000
"You're running out of room here,"

00:46:09.000 --> 00:46:12.240
that strikes me as the worst
sort of loser think of the day.

00:46:12.240 --> 00:46:13.690
Like people have tricked everybody

00:46:13.690 --> 00:46:16.570
into thinking that that diversity matters.

00:46:16.570 --> 00:46:18.530
- You know, I surprise people

00:46:18.530 --> 00:46:20.760
when I tell them that I'm not...

00:46:20.760 --> 00:46:22.940
I don't have a feeling about those times

00:46:22.940 --> 00:46:24.718
that you would expect.

00:46:24.718 --> 00:46:28.580
I don't feel like society made a mistake.

00:46:28.580 --> 00:46:30.680
Because sometimes you
need to use a hammer,

00:46:30.680 --> 00:46:32.960
and sometimes you need to use a scalpel,

00:46:32.960 --> 00:46:34.350
and you have to know the difference.

00:46:34.350 --> 00:46:38.000
In those days when there was
literally just no diversity

00:46:38.000 --> 00:46:41.410
in senior management of two
major companies in the Bay Area,

00:46:41.410 --> 00:46:43.400
for the newspaper to get on them about it,

00:46:43.400 --> 00:46:45.850
I think that's how it happened,
the newspaper got on them,

00:46:45.850 --> 00:46:47.180
perfectly appropriate.

00:46:47.180 --> 00:46:49.147
And for them to say, "Look,

00:46:49.147 --> 00:46:51.897
"this is a sledgehammer situation,

00:46:51.897 --> 00:46:54.140
"and today you're the nail."

00:46:54.140 --> 00:46:56.120
I remember thinking of it as like,

00:46:56.120 --> 00:46:58.880
that's actually a pretty
good societal goal.

00:46:58.880 --> 00:47:01.500
And in fact we're much
closer to that reality,

00:47:01.500 --> 00:47:03.170
far better diversity.

00:47:03.170 --> 00:47:04.410
I think we're better off.

00:47:04.410 --> 00:47:05.480
I think it was good.

00:47:05.480 --> 00:47:07.540
I just happened to be on the wrong side

00:47:07.540 --> 00:47:09.440
of the sledgehammer at that.

00:47:09.440 --> 00:47:11.310
So, while it was bad for me,

00:47:11.310 --> 00:47:14.810
I don't have an activist opinion

00:47:14.810 --> 00:47:16.150
that it shouldn't have happened

00:47:16.150 --> 00:47:18.070
because I think it got
us to a better place.

00:47:18.070 --> 00:47:20.200
- That's interesting,
but do you think that

00:47:20.200 --> 00:47:21.410
part of the problem is that

00:47:21.410 --> 00:47:24.370
we take a sledgehammer to everything now?

00:47:24.370 --> 00:47:26.470
I don't fully agree with the premise there

00:47:27.891 --> 00:47:29.940
because I just think those
things are bad no matter what.

00:47:29.940 --> 00:47:33.120
It's just too against the
individual for my personal taste.

00:47:33.120 --> 00:47:34.940
But I'll go with you.

00:47:34.940 --> 00:47:36.280
Do you think that the problem now

00:47:36.280 --> 00:47:37.990
is that so much has changed...

00:47:37.990 --> 00:47:39.980
We have diversity pretty much everywhere.

00:47:39.980 --> 00:47:42.020
In many ways it benefits you.

00:47:42.020 --> 00:47:43.440
And I'm not talking about
diversity of thought.

00:47:43.440 --> 00:47:44.390
I'm talking about diversity

00:47:44.390 --> 00:47:46.650
of your color and gender
and the rest of it.

00:47:46.650 --> 00:47:48.100
But we're still always hitting everything

00:47:48.100 --> 00:47:49.180
with a sledgehammer now,

00:47:49.180 --> 00:47:52.910
and we don't go in with
the sort of softer tools.

00:47:52.910 --> 00:47:54.210
- I'm very much...

00:47:55.540 --> 00:47:57.150
I guess my feeling is that

00:47:57.150 --> 00:48:00.180
if you've got slavery, you
need your biggest weapon,

00:48:00.180 --> 00:48:01.490
the Civil War.

00:48:01.490 --> 00:48:03.150
If you've got no civil rights

00:48:03.150 --> 00:48:04.680
then you need the civil right movement

00:48:04.680 --> 00:48:05.513
and march in the streets.

00:48:05.513 --> 00:48:07.600
It's smaller than the nuclear weapon.

00:48:07.600 --> 00:48:09.280
And I think we've gotten to the point

00:48:09.280 --> 00:48:12.280
where you're talking about
things like institutional racism.

00:48:13.413 --> 00:48:17.270
In which case, my preference
is to teach strategy.

00:48:17.270 --> 00:48:20.000
Because there does exist

00:48:20.000 --> 00:48:21.910
some amount of bias, et cetera,

00:48:21.910 --> 00:48:23.700
that will affect some amount of people.

00:48:23.700 --> 00:48:25.880
But we now live in a world

00:48:25.880 --> 00:48:28.100
where everybody can find a place

00:48:28.100 --> 00:48:30.796
where the bias works for them. (chuckles)

00:48:30.796 --> 00:48:34.930
There's no Fortune 500
company who doesn't wanna hire

00:48:34.930 --> 00:48:39.930
a qualified woman, black
person, gay, LGBTQ, you name it.

00:48:40.270 --> 00:48:41.650
They all want them.

00:48:41.650 --> 00:48:42.820
If you walk in the door

00:48:42.820 --> 00:48:46.520
and you've got the right
degree, right experience,

00:48:46.520 --> 00:48:49.690
and you say, "And also," you got a job.

00:48:49.690 --> 00:48:52.670
So if you're not teaching strategy

00:48:52.670 --> 00:48:53.830
instead of worrying about this

00:48:53.830 --> 00:48:55.710
one place you can't get a job,

00:48:55.710 --> 00:48:56.730
leave there.

00:48:56.730 --> 00:48:57.940
- How do you move, then?

00:48:57.940 --> 00:49:00.350
If you've succeeded using the hammer,

00:49:00.350 --> 00:49:02.410
it's hard to give up that hammer, right?

00:49:02.410 --> 00:49:04.660
- Well, in the political terms,

00:49:04.660 --> 00:49:06.680
going big is always good.

00:49:06.680 --> 00:49:10.030
You wanna be AOC if you're
gonna be the new politician.

00:49:10.030 --> 00:49:11.477
It's like, "What's the
biggest thing I can say

00:49:11.477 --> 00:49:13.247
"to set everybody's hair on fire?

00:49:13.247 --> 00:49:14.480
"And then if I need to back up..."

00:49:14.480 --> 00:49:16.290
- The world's ending in
12 years, don't forget it.

00:49:16.290 --> 00:49:17.550
- That's pretty good.

00:49:17.550 --> 00:49:18.690
Persuasion wise, pretty good.

00:49:18.690 --> 00:49:20.840
And then you shrink it
back as you need it.

00:49:22.400 --> 00:49:24.010
Try not to...

00:49:24.010 --> 00:49:28.360
Try not to confuse the
hyperbole and the politics

00:49:28.360 --> 00:49:29.700
with what's the right tool.

00:49:29.700 --> 00:49:30.568
- Right.

00:49:30.568 --> 00:49:33.830
Do you think AOC is just
some other version of Trump,

00:49:33.830 --> 00:49:37.290
some reverse version of Trump,
just using those same things?

00:49:37.290 --> 00:49:39.070
