00:54:19.280 --> 00:54:20.490
And Bernie, by the way,

00:54:20.490 --> 00:54:22.180
I have so much respect for Bernie

00:54:22.180 --> 00:54:23.810
while hating his policies.

00:54:23.810 --> 00:54:25.560
Man, that guy's a force of nature.

00:54:25.560 --> 00:54:28.070
He really changed the conversation.

00:54:28.070 --> 00:54:30.500
I don't want it to go
where he wants it to go,

00:54:30.500 --> 00:54:32.090
but I love the fact that

00:54:32.090 --> 00:54:34.660
he's gonna introduce this body of thought.

00:54:34.660 --> 00:54:37.260
- All right, all this sensible talk aside,

00:54:37.260 --> 00:54:39.110
let's shift, for these last few minutes,

00:54:39.110 --> 00:54:40.383
into the simulation.

00:54:41.340 --> 00:54:43.700
I see this conversation
happening a little bit

00:54:43.700 --> 00:54:45.960
here and there, and on some
other podcasts and things.

00:54:45.960 --> 00:54:48.150
I don't know that we've
really ever talked about it

00:54:48.150 --> 00:54:49.730
on this show before.

00:54:49.730 --> 00:54:51.923
What do you mean, the world
could be a simulation?

00:54:51.923 --> 00:54:53.450
What is all this crazy talk?

00:54:53.450 --> 00:54:54.690
- Let me say, first of all,

00:54:54.690 --> 00:54:58.210
that smart people also agree with this.

00:54:58.210 --> 00:55:01.040
Elon Musk, for example,
has a similar view.

00:55:01.040 --> 00:55:04.000
This comes from Nick Bostrom, a physicist.

00:55:04.000 --> 00:55:05.100
So serious people.

00:55:05.100 --> 00:55:08.710
And in fact, in Silicon
Valley, near where I am,

00:55:08.710 --> 00:55:10.900
it's very common for people
to believe this is true,

00:55:10.900 --> 00:55:12.790
but you don't talk about it too much.

00:55:12.790 --> 00:55:13.680
And you're gonna find out why.

00:55:13.680 --> 00:55:14.930
- Probably dinner parties, yeah.

00:55:14.930 --> 00:55:16.650
- You'll find out why
people don't talk about it.

00:55:16.650 --> 00:55:18.710
The idea is that we,

00:55:18.710 --> 00:55:21.890
our current world is approaching a place

00:55:21.890 --> 00:55:24.590
where we could create
a software simulation

00:55:24.590 --> 00:55:26.360
of little creatures in a simulated world

00:55:26.360 --> 00:55:28.140
that are just software

00:55:28.140 --> 00:55:30.550
who would act and believe,

00:55:30.550 --> 00:55:33.410
if you could use that
word, that they're real.

00:55:33.410 --> 00:55:35.380
Now, maybe we're not quite there,

00:55:35.380 --> 00:55:36.650
but you could certainly imagine,

00:55:36.650 --> 00:55:38.860
in your lifetime, we'll be there, easily.

00:55:38.860 --> 00:55:40.173
Maybe 10 years, 20 years.

00:55:41.640 --> 00:55:44.073
The thinking is that if it ever happens,

00:55:45.070 --> 00:55:48.300
by any civilization, it
will happen lots of times.

00:55:48.300 --> 00:55:50.460
And if it's such a good simulation

00:55:50.460 --> 00:55:52.430
the creatures in the simulation

00:55:52.430 --> 00:55:54.480
will someday create their own simulation.

00:55:55.410 --> 00:55:56.620
Now, what would you look for

00:55:56.620 --> 00:55:58.700
to find out if we're a software simulation

00:55:58.700 --> 00:56:01.530
who thinks we're real,
or an original species?

00:56:01.530 --> 00:56:04.530
Well, here are some of the
things you'd do if it's software.

00:56:05.640 --> 00:56:06.610
First thing you'd do is

00:56:06.610 --> 00:56:09.430
you would not create all of the history.

00:56:09.430 --> 00:56:11.640
You would create it on demand.

00:56:11.640 --> 00:56:13.670
For example, if we
discovered a new planet,

00:56:13.670 --> 00:56:16.270
that would never be
written until we see it.

00:56:16.270 --> 00:56:20.140
Now, in physics, can we
show that an observation

00:56:20.140 --> 00:56:22.730
causes a wave to collapse?

00:56:22.730 --> 00:56:25.048
I don't know what I'm talking
about right now, by the way.

00:56:25.048 --> 00:56:27.000
(Dave laughing)

00:56:27.000 --> 00:56:28.450
I hope nobody's watching this

00:56:29.695 --> 00:56:31.210
because when I get into
physics it gets all--

00:56:31.210 --> 00:56:32.043
- Yeah, yeah.

00:56:32.043 --> 00:56:33.290
You had me til wave collapse.

00:56:33.290 --> 00:56:36.200
- But it's generally true
that you need a subjective,

00:56:36.200 --> 00:56:38.150
or a machine, observation of something.

00:56:38.150 --> 00:56:38.983
- Pause for one second.

00:56:38.983 --> 00:56:40.060
You have a little spit over here.

00:56:40.060 --> 00:56:40.893
Let's just...

00:56:41.960 --> 00:56:42.793
Yeah, okay.

00:56:45.767 --> 00:56:46.610
All right.

00:56:46.610 --> 00:56:48.203
Jump from wave collapse.

00:56:49.470 --> 00:56:51.740
- The idea is that, even in physics,

00:56:51.740 --> 00:56:53.510
we know that you need an observer,

00:56:53.510 --> 00:56:55.280
or a machine observer, I think,

00:56:55.280 --> 00:56:56.690
to collapse reality.

00:56:56.690 --> 00:57:00.010
In other words, things don't
exist except in probability,

00:57:00.010 --> 00:57:01.500
on the quantum level,

00:57:01.500 --> 00:57:03.693
as if I know what I'm
talking about here, again,

00:57:04.940 --> 00:57:05.940
until somebody observes them.

00:57:05.940 --> 00:57:08.010
So that's exactly how
you would build software.

00:57:08.010 --> 00:57:09.580
It doesn't exist until you see it.

00:57:09.580 --> 00:57:10.840
You would also say, all right,

00:57:10.840 --> 00:57:13.700
you little software
people can't get outside

00:57:13.700 --> 00:57:15.210
the reality that we defined.

00:57:15.210 --> 00:57:17.410
Because if you could, you
could see what you're in.

00:57:17.410 --> 00:57:19.510
Can you get to the edge of the universe?

00:57:19.510 --> 00:57:20.700
You can't.
- You'll never get there.

00:57:20.700 --> 00:57:24.598
- Because speed of light will
keep you from ever doing it.

00:57:24.598 --> 00:57:27.290
So you see all of these coincidences

00:57:27.290 --> 00:57:30.780
that are exactly compatible
with how a software programmer

00:57:30.780 --> 00:57:32.000
would program a world.

00:57:32.000 --> 00:57:33.440
Then you look at the odds.

00:57:33.440 --> 00:57:35.090
The odds of being an original

00:57:35.090 --> 00:57:37.360
when there will be so many copies,

00:57:37.360 --> 00:57:40.070
one in a trillion, one in a million?

00:57:40.070 --> 00:57:40.903
We don't know.

00:57:40.903 --> 00:57:42.440
Maybe it's one in three.

00:57:42.440 --> 00:57:46.670
But the odds are certainly
much favoring the simulation

00:57:46.670 --> 00:57:49.230
and not that we're an original species.

00:57:49.230 --> 00:57:52.777
- So basically that's
the plot of "The Matrix".

00:57:53.730 --> 00:57:56.524
If we had to whittle
this into something here.

00:57:56.524 --> 00:58:01.500
That we're sort of the batteries
for this digital thing.

00:58:01.500 --> 00:58:03.580
- In "The Matrix" they're real people,

00:58:03.580 --> 00:58:05.260
and they're also a simulation,

00:58:05.260 --> 00:58:07.210
and they're sort of coexisting.

00:58:07.210 --> 00:58:09.580
In this, we're just a simulation.

00:58:09.580 --> 00:58:10.533
- Would it matter?

00:58:11.443 --> 00:58:12.400
You know what I mean?

00:58:12.400 --> 00:58:14.690
Let's say we found out
right now, we're in it.

00:58:14.690 --> 00:58:18.090
Let's say we became self-aware
within the simulation.

00:58:18.090 --> 00:58:20.020
Well, we're still in the
simulation no matter what.

00:58:20.020 --> 00:58:22.540
I guess that could alter
how we would deal with

00:58:22.540 --> 00:58:24.430
the simulations we would
create going forward.

00:58:24.430 --> 00:58:25.350
- Let me ask you this.

00:58:25.350 --> 00:58:27.650
And this is just for fun.

00:58:27.650 --> 00:58:30.180
Don't anybody take this too seriously.

00:58:30.180 --> 00:58:31.690
One of the things you might imagine

00:58:31.690 --> 00:58:34.600
is if somebody realized
they're in a simulation,

00:58:34.600 --> 00:58:35.800
that they could hack it.

00:58:36.990 --> 00:58:39.093
Who believes in the simulation?

00:58:39.970 --> 00:58:41.320
Elon Musk.

00:58:41.320 --> 00:58:42.600
How's he doing?

00:58:42.600 --> 00:58:43.920
(chuckling)

00:58:43.920 --> 00:58:45.375
- Ah, I see what you're saying here.

00:58:45.375 --> 00:58:47.210
- He's sending a rocket
to Mars, for God's sakes.

00:58:47.210 --> 00:58:49.010
- He put a Tesla in space
just because he could.

00:58:49.010 --> 00:58:50.330
